1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,312
Chapter 30: Cesarean Delivery and Peripartum.

3
00:00:06,312 --> 00:00:08,887
Hysterectomy Definitions.

4
00:00:08,887 --> 00:00:11,487
Cesarean Delivery Risks.

5
00:00:11,487 --> 00:00:13,625
Patient Preparation.

6
00:00:13,625 --> 00:00:16,337
Cesarean Delivery Technique.

7
00:00:16,337 --> 00:00:18,162
Laparotomy.

8
00:00:18,162 --> 00:00:19,962
Hysterotomy.

9
00:00:19,962 --> 00:00:22,400
Peripartum Hysterectomy.

10
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:24,487
Postoperative Care.

11
00:00:24,487 --> 00:00:26,250
References.

12
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:36,812
In the United States, the cesarean delivery rate rose most dramatically from 4.5 percent in 1970 to 32.9 percent in 2009.

13
00:00:36,812 --> 00:00:42,287
The rate has since plateaued and was 31.9 percent in 2018 .

14
00:00:42,287 --> 00:00:47,912
Some indications for performing cesarean delivery are shown in Table 30-1.

15
00:00:47,912 --> 00:01:00,062
More than 85 percent of these operations are performed for four reasons—prior cesarean delivery, labor dystocia or arrest, fetal jeopardy, or abnormal fetal presentation.

16
00:01:00,062 --> 00:01:09,237
The latter three compose the main indications for primary cesarean delivery (Barber, 2011; Boyle, 2013).

17
00:01:09,237 --> 00:01:20,000
Efforts to lower these rates are outlined in Safe Prevention of the Primary Cesarean Delivery by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b).

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,250
Table 30-1.

19
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:41,200
Some Indications for Cesarean Delivery Reasons for persistently elevated cesarean rates are not completely understood, but some explanations include the following: 1. Women are having fewer children, thus, a greater percentage of births are among nulliparas, who are at increased risk for cesarean delivery.

20
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:49,562
2. Average maternal age is rising, and older women, especially older nulliparas, have a higher cesarean delivery risk.

21
00:01:49,562 --> 00:01:59,700
3. Electronic fetal monitoring use is widespread and associated with a higher cesarean delivery rate compared with intermittent fetal heart rate auscultation.

22
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:04,037
4. Most breech fetuses are now delivered by cesarean.

23
00:02:04,037 --> 00:02:08,875
5. The frequency of operative vaginal delivery has declined.

24
00:02:08,875 --> 00:02:15,200
6. Obesity, which is a cesarean delivery risk, has reached epidemic proportions.

25
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:23,975
7. Rates of cesarean delivery in women with preeclampsia have risen, whereas labor induction rates for these patients have declined.

26
00:02:23,975 --> 00:02:35,237
8. The vaginal birth after cesarean (VBAC) rate has decreased from a high of 28 percent in 1996 to 13.3 percent in 2018 .

27
00:02:35,237 --> 00:02:42,650
9. Assisted reproductive technology is more widely used and is linked with greater cesarean delivery rates .

28
00:02:42,650 --> 00:02:44,500
Definitions.

29
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:50,262
Cesarean delivery defines the birth of a fetus by laparotomy and then hysterotomy.

30
00:02:50,262 --> 00:02:58,675
This definition is not applied to removal of the fetus from the abdominal cavity in the case of uterine rupture or of abdominal pregnancy.

31
00:02:58,675 --> 00:03:08,137
Rarely, hysterotomy is performed in a woman who has just died or in whom death is expected soon—postmortem or perimortem cesarean delivery .

32
00:03:08,137 --> 00:03:13,325
In some cases, abdominal hysterectomy is indicated after delivery.

33
00:03:13,325 --> 00:03:19,337
When performed at the time of cesarean delivery, the operation is cesarean hysterectomy.

34
00:03:19,337 --> 00:03:24,375
If done shortly after vaginal delivery, it is postpartum hysterectomy.

35
00:03:24,375 --> 00:03:28,887
Peripartum hysterectomy is a broader term that combines these two.

36
00:03:28,887 --> 00:03:35,062
In most cases, total hysterectomy is performed and removes the uterine body and cervix.

37
00:03:35,062 --> 00:03:40,987
Supracervical hysterectomy is selected less often and removes only the uterine body.

38
00:03:40,987 --> 00:03:44,650
With either, adnexa are not usually excised.

39
00:03:44,650 --> 00:03:51,287
In most instances, a simple hysterectomy, which is also a type I hysterectomy, is performed.

40
00:03:51,287 --> 00:04:03,025
However, for women with invasive cervical cancer, radical hysterectomy removes the uterus, parametrium, and proximal vagina to achieve tumor excision with negative margins.

41
00:04:03,025 --> 00:04:11,237
For cases of placenta percreta that extend toward the pelvic sidewall, similar radical excision of the parametrium may be needed.

42
00:04:11,237 --> 00:04:13,837
Cesarean Delivery Risks.

43
00:04:13,837 --> 00:04:21,562
To provide accurate informed consent, knowledge of maternal and neonatal risks and benefits with surgery is essential.

44
00:04:21,562 --> 00:04:31,537
In broad terms, cesarean delivery generally has higher maternal surgical risks for the current and subsequent pregnancies compared with spontaneous vaginal birth.

45
00:04:31,537 --> 00:04:37,675
This is balanced against lower rates of perineal injury and short-term pelvic floor disorders.

46
00:04:37,675 --> 00:04:46,825
For the neonate, cesarean delivery generally offers lower rates of birth trauma and stillbirth but greater rates of initial respiratory difficulties.

47
00:04:46,825 --> 00:04:50,300
# Maternal Mortality and Morbidity.

48
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:56,437
For the mother, death attributable solely to cesarean delivery is rare in the United States.

49
00:04:56,437 --> 00:04:59,450
A review of nearly 1.5

50
00:04:59,620 --> 00:05:10,245
million pregnancies found maternal mortality rates of 2.2 per 100,000 cesarean deliveries compared with 0.2 per 100,000 vaginal births .

51
00:05:10,195 --> 00:05:25,245
In a metaanalysis of 203 studies, Guise and coworkers (2010) reported a maternal mortality rate of 13 per 100,000 with elective repeat cesarean compared with 4 per 100,000 with trial of labor.

52
00:05:25,245 --> 00:05:31,145
Some element of this risk may stem from the underlying indication for the cesarean delivery.

53
00:05:31,145 --> 00:05:39,982
For example, long first-stage labor increases not only cesarean risk but also rates of maternal fever and postpartum hemorrhage .

54
00:05:39,982 --> 00:05:55,982
Additionally, in a study of more than 100,000 deliveries in China, where cesarean birth without a medical indication is common, the maternal mortality rate was not higher in those with nonindicated cesarean delivery than in women with spontaneous vaginal birth .

55
00:05:55,982 --> 00:06:03,632
Similar to mortality rates, the overall frequencies of some maternal morbidities are increased with cesarean delivery.

56
00:06:03,632 --> 00:06:12,570
Villar and associates (2007) reported that maternal morbidity rates were twofold higher with cesarean delivery than with vaginal birth.

57
00:06:12,570 --> 00:06:18,595
Principal among these are infection, hemorrhage, and venous thromboembolism (VTE).

58
00:06:18,595 --> 00:06:31,857
In addition, anesthetic complications, which also rarely include death, have a greater incidence with cesarean delivery compared with vaginal birth (Cheesman, 2009; Hawkins, 2011).

59
00:06:31,857 --> 00:06:39,020
Adjacent organs infrequently may be injured, which is described in detail later (p. 563).

60
00:06:39,020 --> 00:06:46,757
Women who undergo a cesarean delivery are much more likely to be delivered by a repeat operation in subsequent pregnancies.

61
00:06:46,757 --> 00:06:58,695
For women undergoing subsequent cesarean, the maternal risks just described are even greater (Cahill, 2006; Marshall, 2011; Silver, 2006).

62
00:06:58,695 --> 00:07:16,120
Specifically outlined in Chapter 31 (p. 580), abnormal placentation, wound and uterine infections, adjacent organ injury, cesarean hysterectomy, and blood transfusion are among the morbidity that accrues with multiple subsequent cesarean operations.

63
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:29,607
As an advantage, cesarean delivery is associated with lower rates of urinary incontinence and pelvic organ prolapse than is vaginal birth (Gyhagen, 2013a,b; Handa, 2011).

64
00:07:29,607 --> 00:07:39,195
Rates of anal incontinence appear uninfluenced by a vaginal or cesarean route (Blomquist, 2018; Nelson, 2019).

65
00:07:39,195 --> 00:07:50,445
In subgroup evaluation, operative vaginal delivery does carry an overall greater anal incontinence risk, which likely stems from higher sphincter injury rates with these procedures .

66
00:07:50,445 --> 00:07:57,582
Pelvic floor advantages persist to some degree over time, but cesarean delivery is not totally protective.

67
00:07:57,582 --> 00:08:11,220
Moreover, longitudinal studies suggest that initial pelvic floor advantages gained from cesarean delivery are lost as women age (Dolan, 2010; MacArthur, 2011, 2013).

68
00:08:11,220 --> 00:08:24,882
During their State-of-theScience Conference, the National Institutes of Health panel (2006) summarized that stress urinary incontinence rates after elective cesarean delivery are lower than those following vaginal delivery.

69
00:08:24,882 --> 00:08:32,132
However, the duration of this protection is unclear, particularly in older and multiparous populations.

70
00:08:32,132 --> 00:08:40,320
Defining women who would be at highest long-term risk for pregnancy-related pelvic floor disorders might allow accurate delivery counseling .

71
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:43,082
# Neonatal Morbidity.

72
00:08:43,082 --> 00:08:52,032
Cesarean delivery is associated with a lower rate of fetal trauma (Linder, 2013; Moczygemba, 2010).

73
00:08:52,032 --> 00:08:59,782
Alexander and colleagues (2006) found that fetal injury complicated 1 percent of cesarean deliveries.

74
00:08:59,782 --> 00:09:10,470
Skin laceration was most common, but others included cephalohematoma, clavicular fracture, brachial plexopathy, skull fracture, and facial nerve palsy.

75
00:09:10,470 --> 00:09:16,945
Cesarean deliveries following a failed operative vaginal delivery attempt had the highest injury rate.

76
00:09:16,945 --> 00:09:22,957
The lowest rate—0.5 percent—occurred in the elective cesarean delivery group.

77
00:09:22,957 --> 00:09:38,195
That said, Worley and associates (2009) found that approximately a third of women who were delivered at Parkland Hospital entered spontaneous labor at term, and 96 percent of these delivered vaginally without adverse neonatal outcomes.

78
00:09:38,195 --> 00:09:42,045
# Cesarean Delivery on Maternal Request.

79
00:09:42,045 --> 00:09:45,695
Some women request elective cesarean delivery.

80
00:09:45,695 --> 00:09:49,782
Data regarding the true incidence of cesarean delivery on

81
00:09:49,948 --> 00:09:53,598
maternal request (CDMR) are poor.

82
00:09:53,548 --> 00:09:57,585
An older estimate in the United States was 5 percent .

83
00:09:57,585 --> 00:10:06,473
Reasons for the request include protection of pelvic floor support, convenience, fear of childbirth, and reduced risk of fetal injury.

84
00:10:06,473 --> 00:10:17,335
One retrospective study of more than 66,000 Chinese parturients compared outcomes of those who elected planned vaginal birth or requested primary cesarean delivery .

85
00:10:17,335 --> 00:10:22,898
Short-term serious maternal morbidity and neonatal mortality rates were similar.

86
00:10:22,898 --> 00:10:33,035
For the newborns, rates of birth trauma, infection, and hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy were low in both groups but statistically lower with cesarean delivery.

87
00:10:33,035 --> 00:10:37,898
Respiratory distress syndrome rates were greater in the CDMR cohort.

88
00:10:37,898 --> 00:10:42,973
A smaller study comparing these two routes of delivery support these findings .

89
00:10:42,973 --> 00:10:53,285
The debate surrounding CDMR includes two medical points: the concept of informed free choice by the woman, and the autonomy of the physician in offering CDMR.

90
00:10:53,285 --> 00:11:02,635
To address this, the National Institutes of Health (2006) held a State-of-the-Science Conference on Cesarean Delivery on Maternal Request.

91
00:11:02,635 --> 00:11:09,985
Notably, most of the maternal and neonatal outcomes examined had insufficient data to permit recommendations.

92
00:11:09,985 --> 00:11:17,573
Indeed, one of the main conclusions of the conference was that more high-quality research is needed to fully evaluate the issue.

93
00:11:17,573 --> 00:11:28,273
Guidelines from the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) note that CDMR should not be performed before 39 weeks’ gestation.

94
00:11:28,273 --> 00:11:36,510
It is ideally avoided in women desiring several children because of the earlier-described morbidity from accruing cesarean operations.

95
00:11:36,510 --> 00:11:38,648
Patient Preparation.

96
00:11:38,648 --> 00:11:41,448
# Delivery Availability.

97
00:11:41,448 --> 00:11:48,935
No nationally recognized standard of care currently dictates the acceptable time interval to begin cesarean delivery.

98
00:11:48,935 --> 00:11:54,035
Previously, a 30-minute decision-toincision interval was recommended.

99
00:11:54,035 --> 00:12:03,298
However, Bloom and colleagues (2006) evaluated nearly 3000 cesarean deliveries performed for emergency indications.

100
00:12:03,298 --> 00:12:12,760
They reported that failure to achieve a cesarean delivery decision-to-incision time <30 minutes was not associated with a negative neonatal outcome.

101
00:12:12,760 --> 00:12:16,585
A subsequent systematic review echoed this finding .

102
00:12:16,585 --> 00:12:29,173
Despite this, when faced with an acute, catastrophic deterioration in fetal condition, cesarean delivery usually is indicated as rapidly as possible, and thus purposeful delays are inappropriate.

103
00:12:29,173 --> 00:12:47,373
The American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) recommend that facilities giving obstetrical care should have the ability to initiate cesarean delivery in a time frame that best incorporates maternal and fetal risks and benefits.

104
00:12:47,373 --> 00:12:49,960
# Informed Consent.

105
00:12:49,960 --> 00:12:54,660
Informed consent is a process and not merely a medical document.

106
00:12:54,660 --> 00:13:07,023
The consenting conversation should enhance a woman’s awareness of her diagnosis and contain a discussion of medical and surgical care alternatives, procedure goals and limitations, and operative risks.

107
00:13:07,023 --> 00:13:14,285
For women with a prior cesarean delivery, the option of a trial of labor should be included for suitable candidates.

108
00:13:14,285 --> 00:13:24,423
For those desiring permanent sterilization or intrauterine device (IUD) insertion, consenting for these can be completed concurrently (Chaps.

109
00:13:24,423 --> 00:13:30,373
38 and 39, pp. 665 and 684).

110
00:13:30,373 --> 00:13:38,510
An informed patient may decline a particular recommended intervention, and a woman’s decision-making autonomy must be respected.

111
00:13:38,510 --> 00:13:48,973
In the medical record, providers should document the reasons for refusal and should note that the intervention’s value and the health consequences of not proceeding with it have been explained.

112
00:13:48,973 --> 00:13:56,560
For Jehovah’s Witnesses, informed consent discussions regarding blood products ideally begin early in pregnancy.

113
00:13:56,560 --> 00:14:05,435
Acceptable blood products vary widely among individual women, and a preoperative checklist of approved products allows superior preparation .

114
00:14:05,435 --> 00:14:13,598
In general, red cells, white cells, platelets, and plasma are considered primary blood components and are declined.

115
00:14:13,598 --> 00:14:18,698
However, certain clotting factors or cell fractions may be acceptable .

116
00:14:18,698 --> 00:14:26,648
Before and after surgery, iron, folate, and, if necessary, erythropoietin are accepted agents to help

117
00:14:26,812 --> 00:14:29,612
maximize hemoglobin levels.

118
00:14:29,562 --> 00:14:35,512
Perioperatively, phlebotomy is limited, and pediatric collection tubes are preferable.

119
00:14:35,512 --> 00:14:47,112
Intraoperative options include prompt treatment of atony to limit blood loss and use of topical hemostatic agents, tranexamic acid, and desmopressin to promote clot formation.

120
00:14:47,112 --> 00:14:51,287
Red blood cell salvage can provide autologous donation.

121
00:14:51,287 --> 00:14:58,499
Normovolemic hemodilution and controlled hypotensive or hypothermic anesthesia can limit red cell losses.

122
00:14:58,499 --> 00:15:10,349
To minimize uncontrolled vessel bleeding, uterine artery embolization, occlusive vascular balloons, and other options are described in Chapter 44 (p. 779) .

123
00:15:10,349 --> 00:15:13,962
# Timing of Scheduled Cesarean Delivery.

124
00:15:13,962 --> 00:15:22,262
With elective delivery before 39 completed weeks, adverse neonatal sequelae from neonatal immaturity are appreciable .

125
00:15:22,262 --> 00:15:28,424
To avoid these, assurance of fetal maturity before scheduled elective surgery is essential.

126
00:15:28,424 --> 00:15:42,699
Supporting criteria include corroborative ultrasound measurements obtained prior to 20 weeks’ gestation, fetal heart tones heard for the past 30 weeks, and passage of 36 weeks since an initial positive pregnancy test result .

127
00:15:42,699 --> 00:15:45,287
# Preoperative Care.

128
00:15:45,287 --> 00:15:52,949
Current perioperative care is guided by evidence-based enhanced recovery after surgery (ERAS) recommendations.

129
00:15:52,949 --> 00:15:58,462
Among these, solid food intake is stopped at least 6 hours before the procedure.

130
00:15:58,462 --> 00:16:05,012
Uncomplicated patients may consume moderate amounts of clear liquids up to 2 hours before surgery .

131
00:16:05,012 --> 00:16:08,062
Bowel preparation is not recommended .

132
00:16:08,062 --> 00:16:16,549
The woman scheduled for cesarean delivery typically is admitted the day of surgery and evaluated by the obstetrical and anesthesia teams.

133
00:16:16,549 --> 00:16:21,349
Recently performed hematocrit and indirect Coombs test are reviewed.

134
00:16:21,349 --> 00:16:26,537
If the latter is positive, compatible blood availability must be ensured.

135
00:16:26,537 --> 00:16:30,837
Regional analgesia is preferred for cesarean delivery.

136
00:16:30,837 --> 00:16:37,174
To minimize the lung injury risk from rare aspiration, gastric acid is ideally buffered.

137
00:16:37,174 --> 00:16:43,862
An oral antacid is consumed shortly before regional analgesia or induction of general anesthesia.

138
00:16:43,862 --> 00:16:49,087
One example is Bicitra, 30 mL orally in a single dose.

139
00:16:49,087 --> 00:16:57,299
This can be coupled with an intravenous (IV) dose of an H2-receptor antagonist, which also raises gastric pH .

140
00:16:57,299 --> 00:17:06,087
Once the woman is supine, a wedge beneath the right hip and lower back creates a left lateral tilt to aid venous return and avoid hypotension.

141
00:17:06,087 --> 00:17:14,262
Data are insufficient to determine the value of fetal monitoring before scheduled cesarean delivery in women without risk factors.

142
00:17:14,262 --> 00:17:19,212
Our practice is to obtain a 5-minute tracing prior to elective cases.

143
00:17:19,212 --> 00:17:25,224
At minimum, fetal heart sounds should be documented in the operating room prior to surgery.

144
00:17:25,224 --> 00:17:32,999
Of further preparations, hair removal at the surgical site does not lower surgical site infection (SSI) rates .

145
00:17:32,999 --> 00:17:41,399
However, if hair is obscuring, it is removed the day of surgery by clipping, which is associated with fewer SSIs than shaving .

146
00:17:41,399 --> 00:17:47,387
Chemical depilation the night before surgery compared with clipping has similar SSI rates .

147
00:17:47,387 --> 00:17:53,949
An electrosurgical grounding pad is placed near the surgical incision and typically on the lateral thigh.

148
00:17:53,949 --> 00:18:09,262
At Parkland Hospital, we typically insert an indwelling bladder catheter to collapse the bladder away from the hysterotomy incision, to avert urinary retention secondary to regional analgesia, and to allow accurate postoperative urine measurement.

149
00:18:09,262 --> 00:18:15,137
Small studies show that catheterization may be withheld in hemodynamically stable women.

150
00:18:15,137 --> 00:18:21,349
Catheter-related discomfort can be avoided, but urinary tract infection rates are not lower .

151
00:18:21,349 --> 00:18:24,849
The risk of VTE is increased with pregnancy.

152
00:18:24,849 --> 00:18:35,474
In the United States in 2014, women with cesarean delivery had a VTE rate of 8 per 10,000, which almost doubled the rate for those with vaginal birth .

153
00:18:35,474 --> 00:18:50,249
Accordingly, for all women not already receiving thromboprophylaxis, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020c) recommends initiation of pneumatic compression stockings before cesarean delivery.

154
00:18:50,249 --> 00:18:54,274
These are usually discontinued once the woman ambulates.

155
00:18:54,274 --> 00:19:05,487
Consensus varies among organizations, and the American College of Chest Physicians suggests only early ambulation for women without risk factors who are undergoing cesarean delivery .

156
00:19:05,487 --> 00:19:06,999
For

157
00:19:07,156 --> 00:19:14,906
women already receiving prophylaxis or those with increased risk factors, they support escalation of prophylaxis.

158
00:19:14,856 --> 00:19:26,718
Last, the Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (2015) is the most conservative and suggest pharmacological prophylaxis for the largest proportion of patients.

159
00:19:26,718 --> 00:19:33,581
These various methods and recommendations are discussed in Chapter 55 (p. 989).

160
00:19:33,581 --> 00:19:41,581
Some women scheduled for cesarean delivery have a comorbidity that requires specific management in anticipation of surgery.

161
00:19:41,581 --> 00:19:52,681
Among others, these include insulin-requiring diabetes, coagulopathy or thrombophilia, chronic corticosteroid use, and significant reactive airway disease.

162
00:19:52,681 --> 00:19:58,156
Surgical preparations are discussed in the respective chapters covering these topics.

163
00:19:58,156 --> 00:20:00,843
# Infection Prevention.

164
00:20:00,843 --> 00:20:09,756
Antibiotic Prophylaxis Cesarean delivery is considered a clean contaminated case, and postoperative febrile morbidity is common.

165
00:20:09,756 --> 00:20:18,656
Numerous trials show that a single dose of an antibiotic given at the time of cesarean delivery significantly decreases infectious morbidity .

166
00:20:18,656 --> 00:20:28,356
Although more obvious for women in active labor who then require cesarean delivery, this practice also pertains to gravidas undergoing elective surgery.

167
00:20:28,356 --> 00:20:38,056
Depending on drug allergies, most recommend a single IV dose of a β-lactam antibiotic— either a cephalosporin or extended-spectrum penicillin.

168
00:20:38,056 --> 00:20:43,906
A 1-g dose of cefazolin (Ancef) is an efficacious and cost-effective choice.

169
00:20:43,906 --> 00:20:52,581
Additional doses are considered in cases with blood loss >1500 mL or with duration longer than 3 hours.

170
00:20:52,581 --> 00:21:06,831
Recommendations for a 2- or 3-g cefazolin dose in obese parturients are conflicting (Ahmadzia, 2015; Maggio, 2015; Swank, 2015; Young, 2015).

171
00:21:06,831 --> 00:21:21,006
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends a 2-g dose for weights ≥80 kg and 3 g for those ≥120 kg (Berríos-Torres, 2017).

172
00:21:21,006 --> 00:21:31,631
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020d) recognizes either dose as suitable for women ≥80 kg.

173
00:21:31,631 --> 00:21:41,081
One pharmacokinetic analysis in obese women showed sufficient tissue levels with a 2-g dose for cesarean deliveries lasting 1.5 hours.

174
00:21:41,081 --> 00:21:46,843
Authors recommended consideration for redosing in obese women if surgeries were longer .

175
00:21:46,843 --> 00:21:54,681
Evidence also supports extending the antibiotic spectrum (Markwei, 2021; Tita, 2008).

176
00:21:54,681 --> 00:22:06,368
One large randomized trial added azithromycin, 500 mg IV, to standard prophylaxis prior to cesarean delivery for women in labor or with ruptured membranes .

177
00:22:06,368 --> 00:22:15,218
Rates of SSIs and endometritis were significantly lower in the extended-spectrum group compared with those in the standard prophylaxis cohort.

178
00:22:15,218 --> 00:22:28,743
In pregnant women with a history of infection with methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA), a single, 15-mg/kg dose of vancomycin can be added to the standard prophylaxis.

179
00:22:28,743 --> 00:22:37,556
Decolonization plays a limited role but may be considered prior to a planned cesarean delivery in women with known MRSA colonization .

180
00:22:37,556 --> 00:22:45,731
Significant β-lactam allergy manifests as anaphylaxis, angioedema, respiratory distress, or urticaria.

181
00:22:45,731 --> 00:22:57,893
This merits prophylaxis with a single, 900mg IV dose of clindamycin combined with a weight-based, 5-mg/kg dose of an aminoglycoside as an alternative.

182
00:22:57,893 --> 00:23:03,193
The 900-mg clindamycin dose is also used for obese patients.

183
00:23:03,193 --> 00:23:08,431
Some studies link higher SSI rates to regimens that lack a β-lactam .

184
00:23:08,431 --> 00:23:16,306
Antepartum penicillin-allergy evaluation by a specialist may redefine the significance of the patient’s prior reaction.

185
00:23:16,306 --> 00:23:22,618
Advantageously, this clarification may remove an allergy label and expanded options .

186
00:23:22,618 --> 00:23:38,456
Antibiotic administration before surgical incision lowers postoperative infection rates without adverse neonatal effects compared with drug administration after umbilical cord clamping (Mackeen, 2014b; Sullivan, 2007).

187
00:23:38,456 --> 00:23:45,268
Prophylaxis is ideally administered within the 60 minutes prior to the start of planned cesarean delivery.

188
00:23:45,268 --> 00:23:49,943
For emergent delivery, antibiotics are given as soon as feasible.

189
00:23:49,943 --> 00:23:56,956
Preoperative preparation of the abdominal wall skin immediately prior to surgery can help prevent SSIs.

190
00:23:56,956 --> 00:24:07,443
Chlorhexidine-alcohol or povidone-iodine solutions are suitable, but data favor chlorhexidine (Hadiati, 2020; Tolcher, 2019).

191
00:24:07,443 --> 00:24:10,218
However, adding a wiping of the

192
00:24:10,372 --> 00:24:15,447
skin with chlorhexidine the night before surgery was no better than placebo .

193
00:24:15,397 --> 00:24:21,234
Once abdominal preparation is completed and dry, surgical drapes cover the patient.

194
00:24:21,234 --> 00:24:26,859
One type has a cut-out window in the lower abdomen that is surrounded by an adhesive border.

195
00:24:26,859 --> 00:24:33,422
Others are adhesive incisional drapes, in which the skin and plastic drape must be incised together.

196
00:24:33,422 --> 00:24:40,634
The latter may slightly raise SSI rates (Eckler, 2019; Hadiati, 2020).

197
00:24:40,634 --> 00:24:53,647
Although not our practice, preoperative vaginal cleansing with a 1percent povidone-iodine scrub lowered endometritis and SSI rates in some studies (Haas, 2020; Roeckner, 2019).

198
00:24:53,647 --> 00:24:59,822
With povidone allergy, 4-percent chlorhexidine gluconate solution is an alternative.

199
00:24:59,822 --> 00:25:09,972
Last, some early evidence may support extending oral antibiotic prophylaxis for 48 hours postcesarean in obese women to lower SSIs .

200
00:25:09,972 --> 00:25:17,272
Antibiotic prophylaxis against infective endocarditis is not recommended for most cardiac conditions.

201
00:25:17,272 --> 00:25:24,172
Exceptions are women with repaired or unrepaired cyanotic heart disease, prosthetic valves, or both.

202
00:25:24,172 --> 00:25:31,409
Prior infective endocarditis or cardiac transplantation with resulting valve regurgitation are others .

203
00:25:31,409 --> 00:25:39,647
Drug infusion is 30 to 60 minutes prior to expected delivery (Table 52-9, p. 935).

204
00:25:39,647 --> 00:25:46,147
Cesarean infection prophylaxis regimens also serve as appropriate endocarditis coverage.

205
00:25:46,147 --> 00:25:55,347
Other Preventions Glycemic control in diabetics lowers wound infection rates and is emphasized in Chapter 60 (p. 1068).

206
00:25:55,347 --> 00:25:58,472
Smoking is another modifiable risk .

207
00:25:58,472 --> 00:26:08,184
Intraoperative normothermia lowers wound infection rates in general surgery cases (Balki, 2020; Berríos-Torres, 2017).

208
00:26:08,184 --> 00:26:12,559
This tenet is logically extrapolated to cesarean delivery .

209
00:26:12,559 --> 00:26:18,597
In children delivered by cesarean, some evidence shows higher asthma and allergy rates .

210
00:26:18,597 --> 00:26:24,684
Gut microbiome differences between vaginal and cesarean birth groups are a suggested cause .

211
00:26:24,684 --> 00:26:36,409
With the hope to improve neonatal microbiota, swabbing the newborn mouth with a gauze that was incubated in the maternal vagina 1 hour before surgery has been described in preliminary studies.

212
00:26:36,409 --> 00:26:48,797
However, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019c) does not encourage this practice due to few data and the potential for transmission of harmful organisms.

213
00:26:48,797 --> 00:26:51,397
# Surgical Safety.

214
00:26:51,397 --> 00:26:57,247
The Joint Commission (2020) describes a protocol to prevent surgical errors.

215
00:26:57,247 --> 00:27:05,097
For cesarean delivery, all relevant documents are verified immediately before surgery, and a “time out” is completed.

216
00:27:05,097 --> 00:27:12,272
The “time out” requires attention of the entire team to confirm that the patient, site, and procedure are correct.

217
00:27:12,272 --> 00:27:25,372
Important discussions also include introduction of the patient-care team members, verification of prophylactic antibiotics, estimation of procedure length, and communication of anticipated complications.

218
00:27:25,372 --> 00:27:34,509
Additionally, requests for special instrumentation should be addressed preoperatively to prevent potential patient compromise and intraoperative delays.

219
00:27:34,509 --> 00:27:38,197
Surgical fires are a specific safety focus.

220
00:27:38,197 --> 00:27:56,259
As prevention, surgeons ideally assess the fire risk at the procedure’s start, allow adequate drying of alcoholbased skin antiseptics, and keep ignition sources off the patient or drapes when not in use (Food and Drug Administration, 2018; Wolf, 2013).

221
00:27:56,259 --> 00:28:01,772
An instrument, sponge, and needle count before and after surgery is essential.

222
00:28:01,772 --> 00:28:08,559
Moreover, active communication during surgery should clearly convey items added to the operative field.

223
00:28:08,559 --> 00:28:16,272
At surgery completion, if counts are not reconciled, portable radiographic imaging for retained objects is done.

224
00:28:16,272 --> 00:28:18,984
Cesarean Delivery Technique.

225
00:28:18,984 --> 00:28:21,284
# Laparotomy.

226
00:28:21,284 --> 00:28:26,597
As with all surgery, a clear understanding of relevant anatomy is essential .

227
00:28:26,597 --> 00:28:34,697
For entry into the abdomen, a suprapubic transverse incision or a midline vertical one is chosen for cesarean delivery.

228
00:28:34,697 --> 00:28:42,709
Suitable transverse incisions are Pfannenstiel or Maylard incisions, and the Pfannenstiel type is selected most frequently.

229
00:28:42,709 --> 00:28:49,609
Transverse incisions follow Langer lines of skin tension, and thus exert less stress on the closed wound.

230
00:28:49,609 --> 00:28:57,297
Compared with vertical ones, Pfannenstiel incisions offer superior cosmesis and lower incisional hernia rates.

231
00:28:57,297 --> 00:28:59,634
The Pfannenstiel incision,

232
00:28:59,788 --> 00:29:08,500
however, is often discouraged for cases in which a large operating space is essential or in which access to the upper abdomen may be needed.

233
00:29:08,450 --> 00:29:18,875
With transverse incisions, because of the layers created during incision of the internal and external oblique aponeuroses, purulent fluid can collect between these.

234
00:29:18,875 --> 00:29:24,963
Therefore, some favor a midline vertical incision for cases with high infection risks.

235
00:29:24,963 --> 00:29:31,675
Emergent entry is typically faster with vertical incision during primary and repeat cesarean delivery .

236
00:29:31,675 --> 00:29:44,300
Last, neurovascular structures, which include the ilioinguinal and iliohypogastric nerves and superficial and inferior epigastric vessels, are often encountered with transverse incisions.

237
00:29:44,300 --> 00:29:53,350
Logically, bleeding, wound hematoma, and neurological disruption may more frequently complicate these incisions compared with vertical ones.

238
00:29:53,350 --> 00:29:57,800
The best incision for the morbidly obese parturient is unclear .

239
00:29:57,800 --> 00:30:03,625
Our preference for women with morbid obesity is a periumbilical midline vertical incision .

240
00:30:03,625 --> 00:30:14,638
The Maylard incision differs mainly from the Pfannenstiel in that the rectus abdominis muscle bellies and their investing rectus sheath are transected horizontally to widen the operating space.

241
00:30:14,638 --> 00:30:24,125
It is technically more difficult due to required muscle cutting and ligation of the inferior epigastric arteries, which lie laterally to these muscle bellies.

242
00:30:24,125 --> 00:30:30,288
Once access is gained, metal handheld retractors provide exposure for hysterotomy.

243
00:30:30,288 --> 00:30:41,125
One metaanalysis with nearly 1700 women showed no lowering of postcesarean SSI rates with a disposable plastic barrier retractor (AlexisO) .

244
00:30:41,125 --> 00:30:51,400
Transverse Incisions With the Pfannenstiel incision, the skin and subcutaneous tissue are incised using a low, transverse, slightly curvilinear incision.

245
00:30:51,400 --> 00:30:59,000
This is made at the pubic hairline, which is typically 3 cm above the superior border of the symphysis pubis.

246
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:05,713
The incision is extended laterally to accommodate delivery —12 to 15 cm is typical.

247
00:31:05,713 --> 00:31:10,538
Sharp dissection is continued through the subcutaneous layer to the fascia.

248
00:31:10,538 --> 00:31:20,788
The superficial epigastric vessels can usually be identified halfway between the skin and fascia, several centimeters from the midline, and these are coagulated.

249
00:31:20,788 --> 00:31:24,500
The fascia is then incised sharply at the midline.

250
00:31:24,500 --> 00:31:37,963
The anterior abdominal fascia is typically composed of two visible layers, the aponeurosis from the external oblique muscle and a fused layer containing aponeuroses of the internal oblique and transverse abdominis muscles.

251
00:31:37,963 --> 00:31:44,538
Ideally, the two layers are individually incised during lateral extension of the fascial incision.

252
00:31:44,538 --> 00:31:56,250
The inferior epigastric vessels usually lie outside the lateral border of the rectus abdominis muscle and beneath the fused aponeuroses of the internal oblique and transverse abdominis muscles.

253
00:31:56,250 --> 00:32:03,650
Thus, although infrequently required, further lateral extension of the fascial incision may encounter these vessels.

254
00:32:03,650 --> 00:32:11,288
In this case, vessels ideally are identified and coagulated or ligated to prevent bleeding and vessel retraction.

255
00:32:11,288 --> 00:32:28,500
Once the fascia is incised, the inferior fascial edge is grasped with Kocher clamps and elevated by an assistant as the operator separates the fascial sheath from the underlying rectus abdominis muscle either bluntly or sharply until the superior border of the symphysis pubis is reached.

256
00:32:28,500 --> 00:32:36,063
Next, the superior fascial edge is grasped and again, separation of fascia from the rectus muscle is completed.

257
00:32:36,063 --> 00:32:44,475
Blood vessels coursing between the sheath and muscles are clamped, cut, and ligated, or they are coagulated with an electrosurgery blade.

258
00:32:44,475 --> 00:32:50,625
Meticulous hemostasis is imperative to lower rates of incisional hematoma and infection.

259
00:32:50,625 --> 00:33:00,850
The fascial separation progresses cephalad and laterally to create a semicircular area above the transverse incision with a radius of approximately 8 cm.

260
00:33:00,850 --> 00:33:04,050
This will vary depending on fetal size.

261
00:33:04,050 --> 00:33:12,313
The rectus abdominis and pyramidalis muscles are separated in the midline to expose the transversalis fascia and peritoneum.

262
00:33:12,313 --> 00:33:19,625
The transversalis fascia and preperitoneal fat are bluntly dissected away to reach the underlying peritoneum.

263
00:33:19,625 --> 00:33:25,750
The peritoneum near the upper end of the incision is opened carefully, either bluntly or by

264
00:33:25,900 --> 00:33:31,137
elevating it with two hemostats placed approximately 2 cm apart.

265
00:33:31,087 --> 00:33:34,412
This upper site lowers cystotomy risks.

266
00:33:34,412 --> 00:33:42,950
The tented fold of peritoneum between the clamps is examined and palpated to ensure that omentum, bowel, or bladder is not adjacent.

267
00:33:42,950 --> 00:33:45,825
The peritoneum is then incised.

268
00:33:45,825 --> 00:33:55,587
As the incision is extended cephalad above the arcuate line, the transverse fibers of the posterior rectus sheath are seen and are cut along with the peritoneum.

269
00:33:55,587 --> 00:34:02,225
The peritoneal incision is then extended downward to just above the peritoneal reflection over the bladder.

270
00:34:02,225 --> 00:34:12,662
Importantly, in women with prior intraabdominal surgery, including cesarean delivery, omentum or bowel may be adhered to the undersurface of the peritoneum.

271
00:34:12,662 --> 00:34:17,975
In women with obstructed labor, the bladder may be pushed remarkably cephalad.

272
00:34:17,975 --> 00:34:25,075
Midline Vertical Incision This incision begins 2 to 3 cm above the superior margin of the symphysis.

273
00:34:25,075 --> 00:34:31,712
It should be sufficiently long to allow fetal delivery, and 12 to 15 cm is typical.

274
00:34:31,712 --> 00:34:39,287
Sharp or electrosurgical blade dissection through the subcutaneous layers ultimately exposes the anterior rectus sheath.

275
00:34:39,287 --> 00:34:44,487
A small opening is made sharply with scalpel in the upper half of the linea alba.

276
00:34:44,487 --> 00:34:48,000
Placement here helps avoid potential cystotomy.

277
00:34:48,000 --> 00:34:56,937
Index and middle fingers are placed beneath the fascia, and the fascial incision is extended first superiorly and then inferiorly with scissors.

278
00:34:56,937 --> 00:35:06,287
Midline separation of the rectus muscles and pyramidalis muscles and peritoneal entry are similar to those just described for the Pfannenstiel incision.

279
00:35:06,287 --> 00:35:08,600
# Hysterotomy.

280
00:35:08,600 --> 00:35:15,987
Most often, the lower uterine segment is incised transversely as described by Kerr in 1921.

281
00:35:15,987 --> 00:35:23,837
Occasionally, vertical incision confined solely to the lower uterine segment may be elected (Krönig, 1912).

282
00:35:23,837 --> 00:35:33,287
In contrast, a classical incision begins as a low vertical incision, which is then extended cephalad into the active portion of the uterine corpus.

283
00:35:33,287 --> 00:35:40,737
Last, a fundal or even posterior uterine incision may be selected for cases with placenta accreta syndrome.

284
00:35:40,737 --> 00:35:47,050
Low Transverse Cesarean Incision For most cesarean deliveries, this incision is preferred.

285
00:35:47,050 --> 00:35:57,537
Compared with a classical incision, it repairs easily, causes less incision-site bleeding, and promotes less bowel or omentum adherence to the myometrial incision.

286
00:35:57,537 --> 00:36:03,937
Located in the inactive segment, it is also less likely to rupture during a subsequent pregnancy.

287
00:36:03,937 --> 00:36:10,400
Before hysterotomy, the surgeon palpates the fundus to identify degrees of uterine rotation.

288
00:36:10,400 --> 00:36:16,775
The uterus may be rotated so that one round ligament is more anterior and closer to the midline .

289
00:36:16,775 --> 00:36:23,462
In such cases, the uterus can be manually reoriented and held to permit centering of the incision.

290
00:36:23,462 --> 00:36:29,012
This avoids incision extension into and laceration of the adjacent uterine artery.

291
00:36:29,012 --> 00:36:34,312
A moist sponge may be used to pack protruding bowel away from the operative field.

292
00:36:34,312 --> 00:36:44,812
The reflection of peritoneum at the upper margin of the bladder and overlying the lower uterine segment is grasped in the midline with forceps and incised transversely with scissors .

293
00:36:44,812 --> 00:36:52,050
Following this initial incision, scissors are inserted between peritoneum and lower-uterinesegment myometrium.

294
00:36:52,050 --> 00:36:56,525
Open scissors are pushed laterally from the midline on each side.

295
00:36:56,525 --> 00:37:02,675
This transverse peritoneal incision extends almost the full length of the lower uterine segment.

296
00:37:02,675 --> 00:37:08,800
As the lateral margin on each side is approached, the scissors are directed slightly cephalad .

297
00:37:08,800 --> 00:37:19,962
The lower edge of peritoneum is elevated, and the bladder is gently separated from the underlying lower uterine segment with blunt or sharp dissection within this vesicouterine space .

298
00:37:19,962 --> 00:37:26,037
This bladder flap creation effectively moves the bladder away from the planned hysterotomy site.

299
00:37:26,037 --> 00:37:33,800
It also helps prevent bladder laceration if an unintended inferior hysterotomy extension occurs during fetal delivery.

300
00:37:33,800 --> 00:37:39,950
If dense adhesions complicate vesicouterine space dissection, sharp dissection is preferred.

301
00:37:39,950 --> 00:37:48,350
If unclear, the bladder and its upper border can be identified by distending or “back filling” it with fluid instilled through a Foley catheter .

302
00:37:48,350 --> 00:37:50,637
Figure 30-1.

303
00:37:50,637 --> 00:37:56,375
The loose peritoneum above the bladder reflection is grasped with forceps and incised with

304
00:37:56,524 --> 00:37:58,736
Metzenbaum scissors.

305
00:37:58,686 --> 00:38:00,924
Figure 30-2.

306
00:38:00,924 --> 00:38:05,336
This peritoneal edge is elevated and incised laterally.

307
00:38:05,336 --> 00:38:07,649
Figure 30-3.

308
00:38:07,649 --> 00:38:14,036
Cross section shows blunt dissection of the bladder off the uterus to expose the lower uterine segment.

309
00:38:14,036 --> 00:38:20,811
In general, this caudad separation of bladder does not exceed 5 cm and usually is less.

310
00:38:20,811 --> 00:38:32,986
However, in instances in which cesarean hysterectomy is planned or anticipated, extended caudad bladder dissection is recommended to aid total hysterectomy and decrease the risk of cystotomy.

311
00:38:32,986 --> 00:38:36,386
Some surgeons do not create a bladder flap.

312
00:38:36,386 --> 00:38:40,599
The main advantage is a shorter skin incision-to-delivery time.

313
00:38:40,599 --> 00:38:48,699
However, data supporting this practice are currently limited (O’Neill, 2014; Tuuli, 2012).

314
00:38:48,699 --> 00:38:50,686
Uterine Incision.

315
00:38:50,686 --> 00:38:54,361
The uterus is entered through the lower uterine segment.

316
00:38:54,361 --> 00:39:03,511
Digital palpation to find the physiological border between firmer upper segment myometrium and the more flexible lower segment can guide placement.

317
00:39:03,511 --> 00:39:07,261
This is often at the level of the bladder flap incision.

318
00:39:07,261 --> 00:39:13,949
For women with advanced or complete cervical dilation, the hysterotomy is placed relatively higher.

319
00:39:13,949 --> 00:39:20,336
Failure to adjust increases the risk of lateral extension of the incision into the uterine arteries.

320
00:39:20,336 --> 00:39:26,211
It may also lead to incision of the cervix or vagina rather than the lower uterine segment.

321
00:39:26,211 --> 00:39:31,111
Such incisions into the cervix distort postoperative cervical anatomy.

322
00:39:31,111 --> 00:39:34,924
The uterus can be incised by various techniques.

323
00:39:34,924 --> 00:39:43,561
Each is initiated by using a scalpel to transversely incise the exposed lower uterine segment for 1 to 2 cm in the midline .

324
00:39:43,561 --> 00:39:47,724
Repetitive shallow strokes help avoid fetal laceration.

325
00:39:47,724 --> 00:39:53,336
As the myometrium thins, a fingertip can then bluntly enter the uterine cavity.

326
00:39:53,336 --> 00:40:03,536
Once the uterus is entered, the hysterotomy is lengthened by simply spreading the incision, using lateral and slightly upward pressure applied with each index finger .

327
00:40:03,536 --> 00:40:18,324
Instead, some evidence supports widening the lower-uterine-segment incision by pulling with fingers in a cephalocaudad direction to help lower hysterotomy extension rates (Morales, 2019; Xodo, 2016).

328
00:40:18,324 --> 00:40:20,674
Figure 30-4.

329
00:40:20,674 --> 00:40:25,886
The myometrium is incised with shallow strokes to avoid cutting the fetal head.

330
00:40:25,886 --> 00:40:28,286
Figure 30-5.

331
00:40:28,286 --> 00:40:36,024
After entering the uterine cavity, the incision is extended laterally with fingers or with bandage scissors (inset).

332
00:40:36,024 --> 00:40:43,686
The goal is to create an incision sufficiently wide to deliver the presenting fetal part yet avoid overextending the incision.

333
00:40:43,686 --> 00:40:50,374
Extensions usually tear downward into the lower uterine segment or laterally into the uterine vessels.

334
00:40:50,374 --> 00:40:54,911
Extensive inferior tears may include the cervix or vagina.

335
00:40:54,911 --> 00:41:09,336
Risk factors for extensions include occiput posterior fetal head position, primary cesarean delivery, and advanced first-stage or second-stage labor (de la Torre, 2006; Karavani, 2020).

336
00:41:09,336 --> 00:41:15,349
Specific maneuvers to ameliorate this last high-risk setting are discussed in the next section.

337
00:41:15,349 --> 00:41:24,986
Alternatively, if the lower uterine segment is thick and unyielding, cutting laterally and then slightly upward with bandage scissors will lengthen the incision.

338
00:41:24,986 --> 00:41:36,074
Importantly, when scissors are used, the index and middle fingers of the nondominant hand are insinuated beneath the myometrium and above fetal parts to help avert fetal laceration.

339
00:41:36,074 --> 00:41:47,761
Comparing blunt and sharp expansion of the initial uterine incision, blunt stretch is associated with fewer unintended incision extensions, shorter operative time, and less blood loss.

340
00:41:47,761 --> 00:41:56,874
However, the rates of infection and need for transfusion do not differ (Asıcıoğlu, 2014; Saad, 2014).

341
00:41:56,874 --> 00:42:04,411
During hysterotomy creation, if the placenta lies in the incision line, it must be either detached or incised.

342
00:42:04,411 --> 00:42:09,699
Placental function is thereby compromised, and thus delivery is expedited.

343
00:42:09,699 --> 00:42:16,286
At times, a low transverse hysterotomy is selected but provides inadequate room for delivery.

344
00:42:16,286 --> 00:42:25,949
In such instances, one corner of the hysterotomy incision is extended cephalad into the contractile portion of the myometrium—a J incision.

345
00:42:25,949 --> 00:42:30,361
If this is completed bilaterally, a U incision is formed.

346
00:42:30,361 --> 00:42:34,986
Last, some prefer to extend in the midline—a T incision.

347
00:42:34,986 --> 00:42:43,361
As expected, these have been linked with higher intraoperative blood loss (Boyle, 1996; Patterson, 2002).

348
00:42:43,361 --> 00:42:46,274
J-, U-, and

349
00:42:46,444 --> 00:42:56,919
T-incisions extend into the contractile portion, and a subsequent trial of labor after cesarean (TOLAC) is more likely to be complicated by uterine rupture.

350
00:42:56,869 --> 00:43:06,831
Similarly, from limited data, inferior hysterotomy extensions also are associated with higher rupture rates, and in our practice these preclude TOLAC .

351
00:43:06,831 --> 00:43:21,531
The Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (2015) notes insufficient evidence to support the general safety of TOLAC in women with a prior significant uterine extension and cautions for decisions to be individualized.

352
00:43:21,531 --> 00:43:30,694
Last, a prior classical incision, described later, or a fundal incision substantially raises the rupture risk during subsequent TOLAC.

353
00:43:30,694 --> 00:43:44,469
Thus, following a primary cesarean delivery complicated by these exceptional incision types, a conversation disclosing these events and their significance should ensue with the patient and are carefully described in the operative report.

354
00:43:44,469 --> 00:43:46,669
Delivery of the Fetus.

355
00:43:46,669 --> 00:43:53,519
With a cephalic presentation, a hand is slipped into the uterine cavity between the symphysis and fetal head.

356
00:43:53,519 --> 00:43:58,081
The head is elevated gently with the fingers and palm through the incision.

357
00:43:58,081 --> 00:44:03,681
Once the head enters the incision, delivery may be aided by modest fundal pressure .

358
00:44:03,681 --> 00:44:06,069
Figure 30-6.

359
00:44:06,069 --> 00:44:08,444
Delivery of the fetal head.

360
00:44:08,444 --> 00:44:15,694
Figures 30-6 to 30-8: Reproduced with permission from Johnson DD: Cesarean delivery.

361
00:44:15,694 --> 00:44:25,006
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

362
00:44:25,006 --> 00:44:29,494
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

363
00:44:29,494 --> 00:44:36,381
After a long labor with cephalopelvic disproportion, the fetal head may be tightly wedged in the birth canal.

364
00:44:36,381 --> 00:44:44,156
This situation raises the risk of hysterotomy extension, of associated blood loss, and of fetal skull fracture.

365
00:44:44,156 --> 00:44:47,219
First, a “push” method may be used.

366
00:44:47,219 --> 00:44:55,006
With this, upward pressure exerted by a hand in the vagina by an assistant will help to dislodge the head and push it cephalad.

367
00:44:55,006 --> 00:45:01,419
If this is anticipated, a patient in frog-leg position may allow easier vaginal access.

368
00:45:01,419 --> 00:45:07,919
Second, as an alternative, a “pull” method grasps the fetal legs and brings them through the hysterotomy.

369
00:45:07,919 --> 00:45:12,306
The fetus is then delivered as one would complete a breech extraction.

370
00:45:12,306 --> 00:45:22,981
Support for this latter approach comes from small randomized trials and retrospective cohort studies (Berhan, 2014; Jeve, 2016).

371
00:45:22,981 --> 00:45:27,231
The main advantage appears to be lower uterine extension rates.

372
00:45:27,231 --> 00:45:34,131
Another pull method attempts to free the head by placing a palm on each fetal shoulder and gently elevating them.

373
00:45:34,131 --> 00:45:37,994
Last, a least-common method uses a “fetal pillow.

374
00:45:37,994 --> 00:45:45,181
This is a distensible intravaginal balloon that is positioned below the head and then inflated to elevate the fetal head.

375
00:45:45,181 --> 00:45:53,969
Evidence for its superiority to the push method is limited and conflicting (Sacre, 2021; Seal, 2016).

376
00:45:53,969 --> 00:46:00,744
Conversely, in women without labor, the fetal head may be unmolded and lack a leading cephalic point.

377
00:46:00,744 --> 00:46:08,544
The round head may be difficult to lift through the uterine incision in a relatively thick lower segment that is unattenuated by labor.

378
00:46:08,544 --> 00:46:14,706
In such instances, either forceps or a vacuum device may be used to deliver the fetal head.

379
00:46:14,706 --> 00:46:20,794
For this, the fetal head is manually grasped and turned to an occiput transverse position.

380
00:46:20,794 --> 00:46:25,219
In this example, the head is left OT (LOT) position.

381
00:46:25,219 --> 00:46:31,394
Two or more fingers of the right hand are introduced inside the hysterotomy and behind the fetal head .

382
00:46:31,394 --> 00:46:35,319
Fingers of the left hand grasp the handle of the forceps.

383
00:46:35,319 --> 00:46:39,944
The blade’s toe is gently introduced between the hand and fetal head.

384
00:46:39,944 --> 00:46:44,931
It is curved inward between the fetal head and the palmar surface of the fingers.

385
00:46:44,931 --> 00:46:52,856
For the upper blade, two or more fingers of the left hand are inserted into the right posterolateral aspect of the hysterotomy.

386
00:46:52,856 --> 00:46:56,219
The forceps handle is grasped by the right hand.

387
00:46:56,219 --> 00:47:02,269
The blade toe is then gently introduced along the posterior left lower uterine segment wall.

388
00:47:02,269 --> 00:47:08,944
It is curved inward between the fetal face and the palmar surface of the fingers (Fig. 308A).

389
00:47:08,944 --> 00:47:13,519
The fingers of the left hand are moved beneath the lower edge of this blade.

390
00:47:13,519 --> 00:47:18,381
Upward pressure against this edge will sweep or wander the blade into position.

391
00:47:18,381 --> 00:47:22,419
As the blade reaches its final position, the shank and

392
00:47:22,588 --> 00:47:29,075
handle come to rest in the midline, and the two handles are articulated (Fig. 30-8B).

393
00:47:29,025 --> 00:47:33,288
Traction is up and out to guide the occiput through the hysterotomy.

394
00:47:33,288 --> 00:47:37,738
Concurrent, gentle fundal pressure can be applied by an assistant.

395
00:47:37,738 --> 00:47:40,075
Figure 30-7.

396
00:47:40,075 --> 00:47:45,475
To place the first forceps blade, the operator’s palm is slipped beneath the head.

397
00:47:45,475 --> 00:47:53,550
It is guided along the palm to ultimately lie across the fetal malar and lower parietal bone as in vaginal forceps application.

398
00:47:53,550 --> 00:47:55,813
Figure 30-8.

399
00:47:55,813 --> 00:48:03,550
A. To apply the second blade, the operator’s hand is inserted into the posterolateral aspect of the hysterotomy.

400
00:48:03,550 --> 00:48:07,213
The forceps blade is slipped inward across the palm.

401
00:48:07,213 --> 00:48:12,675
This hand then guides the blade to overlie the upper malar and parietal bones.

402
00:48:12,675 --> 00:48:16,450
B. Once positioned, the handles are interlocked.

403
00:48:16,450 --> 00:48:21,313
Slight upward and outward traction is used to lift the head through the incision.

404
00:48:21,313 --> 00:48:29,825
After head delivery, a finger is passed across the fetal neck to determine whether it is encircled by one or more umbilical cord loops.

405
00:48:29,825 --> 00:48:32,963
If present, these are slipped over the head.

406
00:48:32,963 --> 00:48:40,263
The head is rotated to an occiput transverse position, which aligns the fetal bisacromial diameter vertically.

407
00:48:40,263 --> 00:48:49,338
The sides of the head are grasped with two hands, and gentle axial traction is applied until the anterior shoulder enters the hysterotomy incision.

408
00:48:49,338 --> 00:48:54,625
Next, by upward axial traction, the posterior shoulder is delivered.

409
00:48:54,625 --> 00:49:01,163
During delivery, abrupt or powerful lateral force is avoided to avert brachial plexus injury.

410
00:49:01,163 --> 00:49:06,163
With steady outward traction, the rest of the body then readily follows.

411
00:49:06,163 --> 00:49:09,113
Gentle fundal pressure may aid this.

412
00:49:09,113 --> 00:49:18,888
With some exceptions, current American Heart Association recommends against routine neonatal suctioning immediately after birth, even with meconium present .

413
00:49:18,888 --> 00:49:26,300
A fuller discussion of this and delayed umbilical cord clamping is found in Chapter 27 (p. 500).

414
00:49:26,300 --> 00:49:38,213
Specific to cesarean delivery, delayed clamping does not lower maternal hemoglobin levels on the first postoperative day (Jenusaitis, 2020; Purisch, 2019).

415
00:49:38,213 --> 00:49:44,200
After the umbilical cord is clamped, the newborn is given to the team member who will conduct care.

416
00:49:44,200 --> 00:49:55,813
Comparing elective cesarean under neuraxial anesthesia and spontaneous vaginal deliveries, studies show that the need for neonatal resuscitation is not practically different between the two .

417
00:49:55,813 --> 00:50:09,638
The American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) recommend that “a qualified person who is skilled in neonatal resuscitation should be in the delivery room.

418
00:50:09,638 --> 00:50:17,213
At Parkland Hospital, pediatric nurse practitioners attend uncomplicated, scheduled cesarean deliveries.

419
00:50:17,213 --> 00:50:24,375
Notably, as anticipated neonatal risks rise, so too should the resuscitative skills of the attendants .

420
00:50:24,375 --> 00:50:35,650
To promote breastfeeding, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) recommends skin-to-skin contact between newborn and mother in the delivery room.

421
00:50:35,650 --> 00:50:49,013
Although most randomized trials focus on vaginal birth, several small studies support such contact following cesarean delivery, and this is our practice (Frederick, 2020; Moore, 2016).

422
00:50:49,013 --> 00:50:52,575
Breastfeeding can be initiated the day of surgery.

423
00:50:52,575 --> 00:50:54,875
Delivery of the Placenta.

424
00:50:54,875 --> 00:50:59,450
The uterine incision is observed for any vigorously bleeding sites.

425
00:50:59,450 --> 00:51:03,650
These should be quickly clamped with Pennington or ring forceps.

426
00:51:03,650 --> 00:51:19,063
Although some surgeons may prefer manual removal of the placenta, spontaneous delivery prompted by some gentle steady cord traction may reduce the risk of operative blood loss and infection (Anorlu, 2008; Baksu, 2005).

427
00:51:19,063 --> 00:51:24,600
Traction is coupled with fundal massage to hasten placental separation and delivery .

428
00:51:24,600 --> 00:51:26,925
Figure 30-9.

429
00:51:26,925 --> 00:51:31,550
Placenta bulging through the uterine incision as the uterus contracts.

430
00:51:31,550 --> 00:51:37,075
A hand gently massages the fundus to help aid spontaneous placental separation.

431
00:51:37,075 --> 00:51:49,588
Immediately after delivery and quick gross inspection of the placenta for missing portions, the uterine cavity is suctioned and wiped out with a gauze sponge to remove avulsed membranes, vernix, and clots.

432
00:51:49,588 --> 00:51:57,713
In the past, doublegloved fingers or ring forceps placed through the hysterotomy incision were used to dilate a closed cervix.

433
00:51:57,713 --> 00:52:01,150
This does not reduce infection or postpartum

434
00:52:01,300 --> 00:52:08,562
hemorrhage rates and is not our practice (Kirscht, 2017; Liabsuetrakul, 2018).

435
00:52:08,512 --> 00:52:19,675
To help prevent uterine atony after birth, an IV infusion containing two ampules or 20 units of oxytocin per liter of crystalloid is infused at 10 mL/min.

436
00:52:19,675 --> 00:52:27,162
Some prefer higher infusion rates, however, nondilute boluses are avoided because of associated hypotension .

437
00:52:27,162 --> 00:52:32,062
Once the uterus contracts satisfactorily, the rate can be reduced.

438
00:52:32,062 --> 00:52:39,737
Although not available in the United States, an alternative is carbetocin, which is a longer-acting oxytocin derivative.

439
00:52:39,737 --> 00:52:44,862
It provides suitable, albeit more expensive, hemorrhage prophylaxis .

440
00:52:44,862 --> 00:52:55,337
Other choices for hemorrhage prophylaxis include misoprostol (Cytotec) and the ergots, namely methylergonovine (Methergine) and ergonovine (Ergotrate).

441
00:52:55,337 --> 00:53:02,737
A combination agent of oxytocin plus ergonovine (Syntometrine) is used outside the United States.

442
00:53:02,737 --> 00:53:09,875
Of agents, the World Health Organization (2018) recommends oxytocin for first-line use.

443
00:53:09,875 --> 00:53:17,987
Last, tranexamic acid (TXA) can be added to a standard oxytocin infusion to help prevent blood loss.

444
00:53:17,987 --> 00:53:27,150
Despite early encouraging evidence, methodology flaws have been cited (Franchini, 2018; Ker, 2016).

445
00:53:27,150 --> 00:53:37,000
With TXA in one large trial, rates of estimated blood loss >1000 mL or red-cell transfusion were lower than with placebo.

446
00:53:37,000 --> 00:53:43,587
But, TXA did not lower rates of common blood loss–related secondary clinical outcomes .

447
00:53:43,587 --> 00:53:50,462
In contrast to prevention, treatment of atony is found in Chapter 42 (p. 734).

448
00:53:50,462 --> 00:53:52,387
Uterine Repair.

449
00:53:52,387 --> 00:53:59,012
After placental delivery, the uterus is lifted through the incision and onto the draped abdominal wall.

450
00:53:59,012 --> 00:54:06,537
We favor exteriorizing and believe a relaxed, atonic uterus can be recognized quickly and massage applied.

451
00:54:06,537 --> 00:54:14,600
Incision and bleeding points are more easily visualized and repaired, especially if extensions were torn or the patient is obese.

452
00:54:14,600 --> 00:54:20,212
Adnexal exposure is superior, and thus, tubal sterilization is easier.

453
00:54:20,212 --> 00:54:26,237
In contrast, some clinicians prefer to close the hysterotomy with the uterus in situ.

454
00:54:26,237 --> 00:54:39,012
Comparing these two approaches, the Coronis Collaborative Group (2013) trial randomly assigned nearly 5000 parturients and found no differences in the endometritis or transfusion rate.

455
00:54:39,012 --> 00:54:46,100
In one large metaanalysis, nausea and vomiting and associated pain rates were comparable with either method .

456
00:54:46,100 --> 00:54:51,587
Before hysterotomy closure, IUD insertion, if planned, is completed .

457
00:54:51,587 --> 00:54:58,325
Our practice is to perform a primary needle and sponge count prior to uterine incision closure completion.

458
00:54:58,325 --> 00:55:04,487
For closure, one end of the uterine incision is grasped to stabilize and maneuver the incision.

459
00:55:04,487 --> 00:55:11,475
The uterine incision is then closed with one or two layers of continuous 0- or no. 1 absorbable suture .

460
00:55:11,475 --> 00:55:19,700
Chromic catgut suture is used by many, but some prefer synthetic delayed-absorbable polyglactin 910 (Vicryl).

461
00:55:19,700 --> 00:55:29,287
In subsequent pregnancy, neither suture type is superior in mitigating against greater rates of adverse pregnancy outcomes such as uterine incision rupture .

462
00:55:29,287 --> 00:55:31,587
Figure 30-10.

463
00:55:31,587 --> 00:55:37,137
The cut edges of the uterine incision are approximated with a running, locking suture.

464
00:55:37,137 --> 00:55:51,037
Single-layer closure is typically faster and is not associated with higher rates of infection or transfusion (CAESAR Study Collaborative Group, 2010; Dodd, 2014; Roberge, 2014).

465
00:55:51,037 --> 00:56:06,000
Moreover, most studies observed that the number of layers does not significantly affect complication rates in the next pregnancy (Chapman, 1997; CORONIS Collaborative Group, 2016; Durnwald, 2003).

466
00:56:06,000 --> 00:56:16,000
One randomized trial with nearly 1600 parturients found a slightly greater formation rate of cesarean scar niches at 3 months after double-layer closure .

467
00:56:16,000 --> 00:56:21,625
At Parkland Hospital, we use a one-layer uterine closure with chromic catgut.

468
00:56:21,625 --> 00:56:26,450
The initial suture is placed just beyond one end of the uterine incision.

469
00:56:26,450 --> 00:56:34,662
A continuous, locking suture line for hemostasis is performed, and each suture penetrates the full myometrial thickness.

470
00:56:34,662 --> 00:56:39,925
The suture line then extends to a point just beyond the incision’s opposite end.

471
00:56:39,925 --> 00:56:45,737
If bleeding sites or defects persist after a single layer, more sutures are required.

472
00:56:45,737 --> 00:56:52,387
Another layer of running suture or individually targeted figure-eight or mattress stitches are options.

473
00:56:52,387 --> 00:56:54,612
Although not our routine

474
00:56:54,772 --> 00:57:03,197
practice, the peritoneum in the anterior cul-desac can be approximated with a continuous 2-0 chromic catgut suture line.

475
00:57:03,147 --> 00:57:15,709
Multiple randomized trials suggest that omission of this step causes no postoperative complications (Grundsell, 1998; Irion, 1996; Nagele, 1996).

476
00:57:15,709 --> 00:57:23,234
If tubal sterilization is planned, it is completed as described in Chapter 39 (p. 683).

477
00:57:23,234 --> 00:57:32,822
Adhesions Following cesarean delivery, adhesions commonly form within the vesicouterine space or between the anterior abdominal wall and uterus.

478
00:57:32,822 --> 00:57:42,984
With each successive pregnancy, the percentage of affected women and adhesion severity rise (Morales, 2007; Tulandi, 2009).

479
00:57:42,984 --> 00:57:53,022
Adhesions can significantly lengthen incision-to-delivery time and total operative time (Rossouw, 2013; Sikirica, 2012).

480
00:57:53,022 --> 00:58:02,309
Rates of cystotomy and bowel injury also are raised because of adhesive disease (Rahman, 2009; Silver, 2006).

481
00:58:02,309 --> 00:58:13,047
Intuitively, scarring can be reduced by handling tissues delicately, achieving hemostasis, and minimizing tissue ischemia, infection, and foreignbody reaction.

482
00:58:13,047 --> 00:58:28,497
Most recent data on short- and long-term outcomes show no benefit to peritoneal closure (CAESAR Study Collaborative Group, 2010; CORONIS Collaborative Group, 2013, 2016; Kapustian, 2012).

483
00:58:28,497 --> 00:58:39,084
Similarly, most studies show no benefit from placement of an adhesion barrier at the hysterotomy site (Edwards, 2014; Kiefer, 2016).

484
00:58:39,084 --> 00:58:48,459
Abdominal Closure Any laparotomy sponges are removed, and the paracolic gutters and cul-desac are gently suctioned of blood and amnionic fluid.

485
00:58:48,459 --> 00:58:55,084
Some surgeons irrigate the gutters and cul-de-sac, especially in the presence of infection or meconium.

486
00:58:55,084 --> 00:59:07,297
Routine irrigation in low-risk women, however, leads to greater intraoperative nausea but not to lower postoperative infection rates (Eke, 2016; Viney, 2012).

487
00:59:07,297 --> 00:59:13,022
Prior to abdominal closure, correct sponge and instrument counts are verified.

488
00:59:13,022 --> 00:59:17,459
The rectus abdominis muscle bellies are allowed to fall into place.

489
00:59:17,459 --> 00:59:24,747
The overlying rectus fascia is closed by a continuous, nonlocking technique with a delayed-absorbable suture.

490
00:59:24,747 --> 00:59:31,509
In patients with a higher risk for infection, monofilament suture may be preferable to braided material.

491
00:59:31,509 --> 00:59:37,534
The subcutaneous tissue usually need not be closed if it is <2 cm thick.

492
00:59:37,534 --> 00:59:51,184
With thicker layers, however, closure is recommended to minimize seroma and hematoma formation, which can lead to wound infection, disruption, or both (Bohman, 1992; Chelmow, 2004).

493
00:59:51,184 --> 01:00:00,384
Adding a subcutaneous drain does not prevent significant wound complications (Hellums, 2007; Ramsey, 2005).

494
01:00:00,384 --> 01:00:08,697
Skin is closed with a running subcuticular stitch of 4-0 delayedabsorbable suture, with adhesive glue, or with staples.

495
01:00:08,697 --> 01:00:26,747
In comparison, final cosmetic results and infection rates appear similar, skin suturing takes longer, but wound separation rates are higher with metal staples (Basha, 2010; Figueroa, 2013; Mackeen, 2014a, 2015).

496
01:00:26,747 --> 01:00:33,809
Poliglecaprone 25 (Monocryl) or polyglactin 910 (Vicryl) are both suitable .

497
01:00:33,809 --> 01:00:45,997
Outcomes with 2octyl cyanoacrylate adhesive (Dermabond) were equivalent to sutures for Pfannenstiel incisions (Daykan, 2017; Siddiqui, 2013).

498
01:00:45,997 --> 01:00:49,734
A sterile thin abdominal wound dressing is sufficient.

499
01:00:49,734 --> 01:01:06,372
In obese women, most evidence favors against a negative-pressure wound vacuum atop the closed skin incision compared with a standard wound dressing to lower wound infection rates (Gillespie, 2021; Hussamy, 2019; Tuuli, 2020).

500
01:01:06,372 --> 01:01:13,247
Joel-Cohen and Misgav Ladach Techniques The Pfannenstiel-Kerr technique just described has been used for decades.

501
01:01:13,247 --> 01:01:17,884
Others include the more recent Joel-Cohen and Misgav Ladach techniques .

502
01:01:17,884 --> 01:01:25,734
These differ from traditional Pfannenstiel-Kerr entry mainly by their initial incision placement and greater use of blunt dissection.

503
01:01:25,734 --> 01:01:35,584
The Joel-Cohen technique creates a straight 10-cm transverse skin incision 3 cm below the level of the anterior superior iliac spines .

504
01:01:35,584 --> 01:01:41,197
The subcutaneous tissue layer is opened sharply 2 to 3 cm in the midline.

505
01:01:41,197 --> 01:01:45,947
This is carried down, without lateral extension, to the fascia.

506
01:01:45,947 --> 01:01:56,622
A small transverse incision is made in the fascia, and curved Mayo scissors are pushed laterally on each side and beneath intact subcutaneous fat to incise the fascia.

507
01:01:56,622 --> 01:01:58,609
With this incision

508
01:01:58,780 --> 01:02:06,355
completed, an index finger from each hand is inserted between the rectus abdominis muscle bellies and beneath the fascia.

509
01:02:06,305 --> 01:02:14,655
One finger is moved cranially and the other caudally, in opposition, to separate the bellies and further open the fascial incision.

510
01:02:14,655 --> 01:02:20,092
Then, a finger from each hand hooks under each belly to stretch the muscles laterally.

511
01:02:20,092 --> 01:02:26,317
The peritoneum is entered sharply, and this incision is sharply extended cephalocaudad.

512
01:02:26,317 --> 01:02:31,880
Entry with the Misgav Ladach technique differs in that the peritoneum is entered bluntly .

513
01:02:31,880 --> 01:02:35,342
Modifications to the Joel-Cohen method abound.

514
01:02:35,342 --> 01:02:43,092
For emergency delivery, we begin along a line somewhat lower on the abdomen and similar to that for Pfannenstiel incision.

515
01:02:43,092 --> 01:02:56,330
For speed, we extend the initial fascial incision bluntly by hooking index fingers in the fascial incision’s lateral corners and pulling laterally (Hofmeyr, 2009; Oloffson, 2015).

516
01:02:56,330 --> 01:03:04,030
Index fingers insinuated between the rectus bellies then move cephalocaudad in opposition to separate the muscle bellies.

517
01:03:04,030 --> 01:03:12,880
Blunt index-finger dissection enters the peritoneum, and again, cranial and caudad opposing stretch lengthens the peritoneal incision.

518
01:03:12,880 --> 01:03:21,192
Last, all the layers of the abdominal wall are grasped together and pulled laterally in opposition to further open the operating space.

519
01:03:21,192 --> 01:03:29,305
These techniques have been associated with shorter operative times and with lower rates of intraoperative blood loss and postoperative pain .

520
01:03:29,305 --> 01:03:37,242
They may, however, prove difficult for women with preexisting anterior rectus fibrosis and peritoneal adhesions .

521
01:03:37,242 --> 01:03:40,655
Classical Cesarean Incision Indications.

522
01:03:40,655 --> 01:03:49,217
This incision is usually avoided because it encompasses the active upper uterine segment and thus is prone to rupture with subsequent pregnancies.

523
01:03:49,217 --> 01:03:55,667
Some indications stem from difficulty in exposing or safely entering the lower uterine segment.

524
01:03:55,667 --> 01:04:06,905
For example, a densely adhered bladder from previous surgery is encountered; a leiomyoma occupies the lower uterine segment; or the cervix is currently invaded by cancer.

525
01:04:06,905 --> 01:04:13,592
Women with prior radical trachelectomy for cervix cancer are usually delivered by classical incision.

526
01:04:13,592 --> 01:04:19,005
Massive maternal obesity can preclude safe access to the lower uterine segment.

527
01:04:19,005 --> 01:04:28,242
A classical incision is also preferred for placenta previa with anterior implantation, especially those complicated by placenta accreta syndrome.

528
01:04:28,242 --> 01:04:37,405
In extreme cases of this, the typical classical hysterotomy may be placed even higher in the uterine body or posteriorly to avoid the placenta.

529
01:04:37,405 --> 01:04:43,655
Fetuses with cephalic presentation are then delivered in a manner similar to total breech extraction.

530
01:04:43,655 --> 01:04:47,880
In other instances, fetal indications dictate the need.

531
01:04:47,880 --> 01:04:57,717
Transverse lie of a large fetus, especially if the membranes are ruptured and the shoulder is impacted in the birth canal, usually requires a classical incision.

532
01:04:57,717 --> 01:05:07,692
Second, with a fetus presenting as a back-down transverse lie, the back precludes easy grasping of a leg through a transverse uterine incision for breech delivery.

533
01:05:07,692 --> 01:05:11,705
Here, a classical incision provides superior room.

534
01:05:11,705 --> 01:05:18,192
Third, in instances when the fetus is very small and breech, a classical incision may be preferable .

535
01:05:18,192 --> 01:05:26,955
In such cases, the poorly developed lower uterine segment provides inadequate space for the manipulations required for breech delivery.

536
01:05:26,955 --> 01:05:34,917
Or, less commonly, the small fetal head may become entrapped by a contracting uterine fundus following membrane rupture.

537
01:05:34,917 --> 01:05:43,692
With multiple fetuses, a classical incision again may provide needed room for extraction of fetuses that may be malpositioned or preterm .

538
01:05:43,692 --> 01:05:50,817
A large fetal malformation or conjoined twins may require the added space afforded by a classical incision.

539
01:05:50,817 --> 01:05:53,455
Uterine Incision and Repair.

540
01:05:53,455 --> 01:06:01,555
A vertical uterine incision is initiated with a scalpel beginning as low as possible and preferably within the lower uterine segment .

541
01:06:01,555 --> 01:06:11,617
If adhesions, insufficient exposure, a tumor, or placenta percreta preclude development of a bladder flap, then the incision is made above the level of the bladder.

542
01:06:11,617 --> 01:06:17,242
Described earlier (p. 553), back filling the bladder may aid this.

543
01:06:17,242 --> 01:06:19,655
Figure 30-11.

544
01:06:19,655 --> 01:06:25,167
An initial small vertical hysterotomy incision is made in the lower uterine segment.

545
01:06:25,167 --> 01:06:26,792
Fingers

546
01:06:26,956 --> 01:06:32,243
are insinuated between the myometrium and fetus to avoid fetal laceration.

547
01:06:32,193 --> 01:06:36,606
Scissors extend the incision cephalad as needed for delivery.

548
01:06:36,606 --> 01:06:44,268
Figures 30-11 and 30-12: Reproduced with permission from Johnson DD: Cesarean delivery.

549
01:06:44,268 --> 01:06:53,581
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

550
01:06:53,581 --> 01:06:58,068
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

551
01:06:58,068 --> 01:07:07,043
Once the uterus is entered with a scalpel, the incision is extended cephalad with bandage scissors until it is long enough to permit fetal delivery.

552
01:07:07,043 --> 01:07:11,856
With scissor use, the fetus can be better protected from laceration.

553
01:07:11,856 --> 01:07:19,081
Fingers of the surgeon’s nondominant hand are insinuated between the myometrium and fetus to prevent scissor cuts.

554
01:07:19,081 --> 01:07:26,393
As the incision is opened, numerous large vessels that bleed profusely are commonly encountered within the myometrium.

555
01:07:26,393 --> 01:07:32,081
The remainder of fetal and placental delivery mirrors that with a low transverse hysterotomy.

556
01:07:32,081 --> 01:07:41,693
For incision closure, one method employs a layer of 0- or no. 1 chromic catgut with a running stitch to approximate the deeper length of the incision .

557
01:07:41,693 --> 01:07:48,656
The outer layer of myometrium is then closed along its length with similar suture and with a running suture line.

558
01:07:48,656 --> 01:08:01,631
To achieve good approximation and to prevent the suture from tearing through the myometrium, it is helpful to have an assistant relieve tension by compressing the uterus on each side of the wound toward the midline as each stitch is placed.

559
01:08:01,631 --> 01:08:04,006
Figure 30-12.

560
01:08:04,006 --> 01:08:06,468
Classical incision closure.

561
01:08:06,468 --> 01:08:13,243
The deeper half (A) and superficial half (B) of the incision are closed in a running fashion.

562
01:08:13,243 --> 01:08:16,393
The serosa is then closed (C).

563
01:08:16,393 --> 01:08:18,831
Peripartum Hysterectomy.

564
01:08:18,831 --> 01:08:21,231
# Indications.

565
01:08:21,231 --> 01:08:35,643
Hysterectomy is most commonly performed to arrest or prevent hemorrhage from intractable uterine atony, surgical trauma/tears, or abnormal placentation (Bateman, 2012; Kallianidis, 2020).

566
01:08:35,643 --> 01:08:42,268
It is more often completed during or after cesarean delivery but may be needed following vaginal birth.

567
01:08:42,268 --> 01:08:57,181
Among all deliveries, the peripartum hysterectomy rate in the United States approximates 1 per 1000 births and has risen significantly during the past few decades (Bateman, 2012; Govindappagari, 2016).

568
01:08:57,181 --> 01:09:05,706
During a 25-year period, the rate of peripartum hysterectomy at Parkland Hospital was 1.7 per 1000 births .

569
01:09:05,706 --> 01:09:17,831
Most of this rise is attributed to the increasing rates of cesarean delivery and its associated complications in subsequent pregnancy (Flood, 2009; Orbach, 2011).

570
01:09:17,831 --> 01:09:28,868
Of hysterectomies, approximately one half to two thirds are total, whereas the remaining cases are supracervical (Rossi, 2010; Shellhaas, 2009).

571
01:09:28,868 --> 01:09:36,318
Major complications of peripartum hysterectomy include greater blood loss and risk of urinary tract damage.

572
01:09:36,318 --> 01:09:46,806
Blood loss is usually appreciable because hysterectomy is being performed for hemorrhage that frequently is torrential, and the procedure itself is associated with substantial bleeding.

573
01:09:46,806 --> 01:09:55,006
Although many cases with hemorrhage cannot be anticipated, those with abnormal implantation are often identified antepartum.

574
01:09:55,006 --> 01:10:07,743
Preparations for placenta accreta syndrome are discussed in Chapter 43 (p. 763) and are outlined by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018).

575
01:10:07,743 --> 01:10:16,231
An important factor affecting the cesarean hysterectomy complication rate is whether the operation is performed electively or emergently.

576
01:10:16,231 --> 01:10:30,556
With anticipated or planned cesarean hysterectomy, rates of blood loss, blood transfusion, and urinary tract complications are lower those with emergent procedures (Briery, 2007; Glaze, 2008).

577
01:10:30,556 --> 01:10:33,393
# Hysterectomy Technique.

578
01:10:33,393 --> 01:10:39,081
Total or supracervical hysterectomy is performed using standard operative techniques.

579
01:10:39,081 --> 01:10:46,943
Adequate exposure is essential, but initially, placement of a selfretaining retractor such as a Balfour is not necessary.

580
01:10:46,943 --> 01:10:56,518
Rather, satisfactory exposure is obtained with cephalad traction on the uterus by an assistant, along with handheld Richardson or Deaver retractors.

581
01:10:56,518 --> 01:11:03,143
The bladder flap is deflected caudad to the level of the cervix if possible to permit total hysterectomy.

582
01:11:03,143 --> 01:11:12,406
In cases in which cesarean hysterectomy is planned or strongly suspected, extended bladder flap dissection is ideally completed before initial

583
01:11:12,556 --> 01:11:14,356
hysterotomy.

584
01:11:14,306 --> 01:11:21,606
Later attempts at bladder dissection may be obscured by bleeding, or excess blood may be lost during this dissection.

585
01:11:21,606 --> 01:11:26,143
After cesarean delivery, the placenta is typically removed.

586
01:11:26,143 --> 01:11:34,756
In cases of placenta accreta syndrome for which hysterectomy is already planned, the placenta is usually left undisturbed in situ.

587
01:11:34,756 --> 01:11:45,693
If the hysterotomy incision is bleeding appreciably, it can be quickly reapproximated with full-thickness sutures, or Pennington or sponge forceps can be applied for hemostasis.

588
01:11:45,693 --> 01:11:49,593
If bleeding is minimal, neither maneuver is necessary.

589
01:11:49,593 --> 01:11:55,831
The round ligament is divided close to the uterus between clamps, and each pedicle is ligated .

590
01:11:55,831 --> 01:12:02,181
Either 0 or no. 1 suture can be used in either chromic gut or delayed-absorbable material.

591
01:12:02,181 --> 01:12:11,181
Division of the round provides access to the anterior leaf of the broad ligament, which is incised downward to meet the former bladder flap incision.

592
01:12:11,181 --> 01:12:22,406
The posterior leaf of the broad ligament adjacent to the uterus is bluntly or sharply perforated at a point beneath the fallopian tube, uteroovarian ligament, and ovarian vessels .

593
01:12:22,406 --> 01:12:28,281
These structures together are then divided between sturdy clamps placed close to the uterus .

594
01:12:28,281 --> 01:12:31,343
The lateral pedicle is doubly ligated.

595
01:12:31,343 --> 01:12:36,618
The medial clamp remains and is removed later with the entire uterine specimen.

596
01:12:36,618 --> 01:12:42,981
The posterior leaf of the broad ligament is then incised caudad toward the uterosacral ligaments .

597
01:12:42,981 --> 01:12:48,468
The bladder and attached peritoneal flap are further deflected and dissected as needed.

598
01:12:48,468 --> 01:12:56,606
If the bladder flap is densely adhered, as it may be after previous hysterotomy incisions, careful sharp dissection is employed .

599
01:12:56,606 --> 01:12:59,156
Figure 30-13.

600
01:12:59,156 --> 01:13:04,168
The round ligaments are clamped, ligated, and transected bilaterally.

601
01:13:04,168 --> 01:13:14,081
Figures 30-13 to 30-21: Reproduced with permission from Cunningham FG, Gilstrap LC III: Peripartum hysterectomy.

602
01:13:14,081 --> 01:13:23,393
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

603
01:13:23,393 --> 01:13:27,881
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

604
01:13:27,881 --> 01:13:30,456
Figure 30-14.

605
01:13:30,456 --> 01:13:40,431
The posterior leaf of the broad ligament adjacent to the uterus is perforated just beneath the fallopian tube, uteroovarian ligaments, and ovarian vessels.

606
01:13:40,431 --> 01:13:42,968
Figure 30-15.

607
01:13:42,968 --> 01:13:47,593
The uteroovarian ligament and fallopian tube are clamped and cut.

608
01:13:47,593 --> 01:13:50,656
The lateral pedicle is doubly ligated.

609
01:13:50,656 --> 01:13:53,193
Figure 30-16.

610
01:13:53,193 --> 01:13:59,368
The posterior leaf of the broad ligament is divided inferiorly toward the uterosacral ligament.

611
01:13:59,368 --> 01:14:01,968
Figure 30-17.

612
01:14:01,968 --> 01:14:06,131
The bladder is dissected sharply from the lower uterine segment.

613
01:14:06,131 --> 01:14:13,593
Special care is required from this point on to avoid injury to the ureters, which pass beneath the uterine arteries.

614
01:14:13,593 --> 01:14:23,143
To help accomplish this, an assistant places constant traction to pull the uterus in the direction away from the side on which the uterine vessels are being ligated.

615
01:14:23,143 --> 01:14:28,006
The ascending uterine artery and veins on either side are identified.

616
01:14:28,006 --> 01:14:31,706
These vessels are then clamped adjacent to the uterus .

617
01:14:31,706 --> 01:14:35,756
For security, some may prefer two lateral clamps.

618
01:14:35,756 --> 01:14:40,118
The medial-most clamp helps prevent back bleeding from the uterus.

619
01:14:40,118 --> 01:14:45,956
The uterine vessels are divided, and the lateral tissue pedicle is doubly suture ligated.

620
01:14:45,956 --> 01:14:50,093
The medial clamp remains for later removal with the specimen.

621
01:14:50,093 --> 01:14:59,468
After securing the uterine vessels on one side, the round ligament, adnexal pedicle, and uterine vessels are then addressed on the contralateral side.

622
01:14:59,468 --> 01:15:02,006
Figure 30-18.

623
01:15:02,006 --> 01:15:04,618
The uterine vessels are clamped.

624
01:15:04,618 --> 01:15:10,718
Once divided, the lateral vascular pedicle is doubly ligated to ensure hemostasis.

625
01:15:10,718 --> 01:15:19,818
In cases with profuse hemorrhage, time and rapid hemostasis can be gained by quickly clamping and dividing all of the just-described pedicles.

626
01:15:19,818 --> 01:15:25,956
Once all are clamped, the surgical team can then return to ligate each pedicle individually.

627
01:15:25,956 --> 01:15:41,318
Total Hysterectomy Even if total hysterectomy is planned, we find it technically easier in many cases to finish the operation after amputating the uterine fundus and placing Ochsner or Kocher clamps on the cervical stump for traction and hemostasis.

628
01:15:41,318 --> 01:15:45,368
Self-retaining retractors also may be placed at this time.

629
01:15:45,368 --> 01:15:49,906
To remove the cervix, the bladder is mobilized further if needed.

630
01:15:49,906 --> 01:15:55,306
This carries the ureters caudad as the bladder is retracted beneath the symphysis.

631
01:15:55,306 --> 01:15:56,756
Bladder

632
01:15:56,908 --> 01:16:04,570
retraction can also help prevent laceration or suturing of the bladder during cervical excision and vaginal cuff closure.

633
01:16:04,520 --> 01:16:14,858
The cardinal ligament, the uterosacral ligaments, and the many large vessels within these ligaments are clamped systematically with sturdy Heaney-type curved or straight clamps .

634
01:16:14,858 --> 01:16:22,183
The clamps are placed as close to the cervix as possible, taking care not to include excessive tissue in each clamp.

635
01:16:22,183 --> 01:16:28,045
The tissue between the pair of clamps is incised, and the lateral pedicle is suture ligated.

636
01:16:28,045 --> 01:16:35,620
These steps are repeated caudally and bilaterally until the level of the lateral vaginal fornix is reached on each side.

637
01:16:35,620 --> 01:16:44,533
In this way, the descending branches of the uterine vessels are clamped, cut, and ligated as the cervix is separated from the cardinal ligaments.

638
01:16:44,533 --> 01:16:47,095
Figure 30-19.

639
01:16:47,095 --> 01:16:51,433
The cardinal ligaments are clamped, incised, and ligated.

640
01:16:51,433 --> 01:16:59,970
If the cervix is effaced and dilated considerably, its softness may obscure palpable identification of the cervicovaginal junction.

641
01:16:59,970 --> 01:17:10,958
The junction location can be ascertained either through the open hysterotomy incision or through a vertical uterine incision made anteriorly in the midline at the level of the ligated uterine vessels.

642
01:17:10,958 --> 01:17:18,520
A finger is directed inferiorly through the incision to identify the free margin of the dilated, effaced cervix.

643
01:17:18,520 --> 01:17:21,420
The contaminated glove is replaced.

644
01:17:21,420 --> 01:17:36,270
Another useful method to identify the cervical margins in cases of planned hysterectomy is to transvaginally place four metal skin clips or brightly colored sutures at 12, 3, 6, and 9 o’clock positions on the cervical edges.

645
01:17:36,270 --> 01:17:46,408
Immediately below the level of the cervix, a curved clamp is placed across the lateral vaginal fornix on each side, and the vagina is incised above the clamp .

646
01:17:46,408 --> 01:17:50,920
The cervix is inspected to ensure that it has been completely removed.

647
01:17:50,920 --> 01:17:56,570
A transfixing suture is used for vaginal cuff closure as each clamp is removed.

648
01:17:56,570 --> 01:18:01,533
Interrupted stitches may be added to approximate the middle portion of the cuff .

649
01:18:01,533 --> 01:18:08,308
Each lateral vaginal fornix is secured to the uterosacral ligaments to mitigate later vaginal prolapse.

650
01:18:08,308 --> 01:18:19,883
For cuff closure, some surgeons instead prefer to close the vagina by apposing the anterior and posterior vaginal walls with interrupted figure-eight sutures or running suture line.

651
01:18:19,883 --> 01:18:22,283
Figure 30-20.

652
01:18:22,283 --> 01:18:31,420
A curved clamp is placed across the lateral vaginal fornix below the level of the cervix, and the tissue incised medially to the point of the clamp.

653
01:18:31,420 --> 01:18:34,008
Figure 30-21.

654
01:18:34,008 --> 01:18:39,408
A transfixing stitch is placed on each side to close the lateral vaginal cuff.

655
01:18:39,408 --> 01:18:44,933
Interrupted stitches (dotted lines) may be needed to close any midline gap.

656
01:18:44,933 --> 01:18:48,158
All sites are examined carefully for bleeding.

657
01:18:48,158 --> 01:18:57,383
One technique is to perform a systematic bilateral survey from the fallopian tube and ovarian ligament pedicles to the vaginal vault and bladder flap.

658
01:18:57,383 --> 01:19:01,433
Bleeding sites are ligated with care to avoid the ureters.

659
01:19:01,433 --> 01:19:09,558
The abdominal wall normally is closed in layers, as previously described for cesarean delivery (p. 558).

660
01:19:09,558 --> 01:19:19,333
Supracervical Hysterectomy To perform a subtotal hysterectomy, the uterine body is amputated immediately above the level of uterine artery ligation.

661
01:19:19,333 --> 01:19:27,233
The cervical stump may be closed with a continuous or interrupted suture line of chromic catgut or delayed-absorbable material.

662
01:19:27,233 --> 01:19:32,308
Subtotal hysterectomy is often all that is necessary to stop hemorrhage.

663
01:19:32,308 --> 01:19:41,295
It may be preferred for women who would benefit from a shorter surgery or for those with extensive adhesions that threaten significant urinary tract injury.

664
01:19:41,295 --> 01:19:52,470
Salpingo-oophorectomy Because of the large adnexal vessels and their close proximity to the uterus, it may be necessary to remove one or both adnexa to obtain hemostasis.

665
01:19:52,470 --> 01:19:59,933
Briery and colleagues (2007) reported unilateral or bilateral oophorectomy in a fourth of cases.

666
01:19:59,933 --> 01:20:05,470
Preoperative counseling for anticipated hysterectomy should include this possibility.

667
01:20:05,470 --> 01:20:08,895
# Urinary Tract or Bowel Injury.

668
01:20:08,895 --> 01:20:12,633
These injuries are rare during cesarean delivery.

669
01:20:12,633 --> 01:20:28,020
The bladder laceration rate approximates 2 injuries per 1000 cesarean deliveries, whereas that for ureteral trauma nears 0.3 events per 1000 cases (Güngördük, 2010; Oliphant, 2014).

670
01:20:28,020 --> 01:20:30,845
Bowel is damaged in about 1 in

671
01:20:31,012 --> 01:20:33,924
1000 cesarean deliveries .

672
01:20:33,874 --> 01:20:49,899
Cystotomy Bladder laceration most commonly occurs during blunt or sharp dissection in the vesicouterine space to create the bladder flap, during peritoneal cavity entry, and during hysterotomy (Phipps, 2005; Rahman, 2009).

673
01:20:49,899 --> 01:21:11,512
Risks are prior cesarean delivery; comorbid adhesive disease; emergency cesarean delivery; cesarean hysterectomy, especially cases with morbidly adherent placenta; and surgery in second-stage labor compared with first-stage (Alexander, 2007; Salman, 2017; Silver, 2006).

674
01:21:11,512 --> 01:21:19,174
Bladder injury is typically identified intraoperatively, and a clear-fluid gush or the Foley bulb are indicators.

675
01:21:19,174 --> 01:21:27,287
In suspected cases, cystotomy can be confirmed with retrograde instillation of fluid through a Foley catheter and into the bladder.

676
01:21:27,287 --> 01:21:31,924
Dilute sterile infant formula is a common, available option.

677
01:21:31,924 --> 01:21:35,137
Methylene blue–stained saline is another.

678
01:21:35,137 --> 01:21:41,499
Leakage of the indicator fluid aids laceration identification and delineation of its borders.

679
01:21:41,499 --> 01:21:50,662
The dome is lacerated most often, and trigone injuries make up the remainder (Phipps, 2005; Salman, 2017).

680
01:21:50,662 --> 01:21:57,337
Prior to cystotomy repair, ureters are examined, and urine jets from each orifice are sought.

681
01:21:57,337 --> 01:22:02,012
Inspection can be done directly through the cystotomy, if at the dome.

682
01:22:02,012 --> 01:22:08,674
A separate diagnostic extraperitoneal cystotomy may be preferable if the laceration nears the trigone.

683
01:22:08,674 --> 01:22:14,662
To aid viewing, urine jets can be colored by IV dye, as described in the next section.

684
01:22:14,662 --> 01:22:24,787
Once ureteral patency is confirmed, the bladder can be closed with a two- or three-layer running closure using a 3-0 absorbable or delayedabsorbable suture .

685
01:22:24,787 --> 01:22:28,474
The first layer inverts the mucosa into the bladder.

686
01:22:28,474 --> 01:22:32,712
Subsequent layers reapproximate the bladder muscularis.

687
01:22:32,712 --> 01:22:38,849
After the final layer, the bladder is filled with a marker fluid to demonstrate repair integrity.

688
01:22:38,849 --> 01:22:43,437
Leaking defects are closed with interrupted reinforcing stitches.

689
01:22:43,437 --> 01:22:45,999
Figure 30-22.

690
01:22:45,999 --> 01:22:48,174
Cystotomy repair.

691
01:22:48,174 --> 01:22:57,137
A. The primary layer inverts the bladder mucosa with running or interrupted sutures of 3-0 delayed-absorbable or absorbable suture.

692
01:22:57,137 --> 01:23:04,149
B. Second and possibly a third layer approximate the bladder muscularis to reinforce the incision closure.

693
01:23:04,149 --> 01:23:10,274
Reproduced with permission from Balgobin S: Urologic and gastrointestinal injuries.

694
01:23:10,274 --> 01:23:19,587
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

695
01:23:19,587 --> 01:23:24,074
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

696
01:23:24,074 --> 01:23:32,987
Postoperative care requires continuous bladder drainage for 7 to 14 days to permit healing and minimize the risk of fistula formation.

697
01:23:32,987 --> 01:23:37,512
Uropathogen prophylaxis during this drainage is not required.

698
01:23:37,512 --> 01:23:46,162
Prior to catheter removal, cystourethrography may not be needed for a simple, single laceration measuring under 1 cm .

699
01:23:46,162 --> 01:23:51,012
Larger lacerations in or near the trigone require careful attention.

700
01:23:51,012 --> 01:23:58,999
Specialists may be consulted, and in preparation, ureteral catheters, described in the next section, can be assembled.

701
01:23:58,999 --> 01:24:03,999
Ureteral orifices are directly inspected to document jets from both.

702
01:24:03,999 --> 01:24:11,374
If not seen, a ureteral catheter may be passed through the cystotomy and into each orifice to confirm patency.

703
01:24:11,374 --> 01:24:19,324
Once this is confirmed, repair should not disrupt the ureteral orifices, and stents may remain to ensure their patency.

704
01:24:19,324 --> 01:24:31,449
Unrecognized cystotomy can manifest postoperatively as hematuria, oliguria, abdominal pain, ileus, ascites, peritonitis, fever, urinoma, or fistula.

705
01:24:31,449 --> 01:24:39,612
For diagnosis, retrograde cystography or abdominal computed tomography (CT) with cystography can be used .

706
01:24:39,612 --> 01:24:44,399
Cystoscopy is also an option but may require a procedural room.

707
01:24:44,399 --> 01:24:48,912
Once cystotomy is identified, prompt repair is indicated .

708
01:24:48,912 --> 01:24:57,687
Ureteral Injury The ureter may be at risk during cesarean hysterectomy, especially those complicated by placenta accreta syndrome.

709
01:24:57,687 --> 01:25:09,637
Although not our practice, some advocate preoperative ureteral catheter placement to aid intraoperative ureter identification (Eller, 2009; Matsubara, 2013).

710
01:25:09,637 --> 01:25:24,699
Organization guidelines recommend individualization of this practice depending on anticipated placenta accreta syndrome complexity (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2018; Collins, 2019).

711
01:25:24,699 --> 01:25:28,562
Further, initial dorsal lithotomy positioning of

712
01:25:28,732 --> 01:25:32,557
the patient for these cases can permit cystoscopy.

713
01:25:32,507 --> 01:25:40,932
Aside from hysterectomy, the ureter is also at risk during repair of hysterotomy extensions into the broad ligament or vagina .

714
01:25:40,932 --> 01:25:48,632
If ureteral injury is suspected, IV dye is administered, and the pelvis is directly inspected for extravasation.

715
01:25:48,632 --> 01:25:54,494
Of options, 50 mg of IV methylene blue may be given over 5 minutes.

716
01:25:54,494 --> 01:26:03,932
However, methylene blue carries the potential for inciting methemoglobinemia in patients with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency.

717
01:26:03,932 --> 01:26:16,382
Another option is 25 or 50 mg of IV 10-percent sodium fluorescein, which stains bright yellow (Espaillat-Rijo, 2016; Grimes, 2017).

718
01:26:16,382 --> 01:26:23,494
Next, brisk dye-stained urine jets are sought from each orifice to exclude ureteral kinking or ligation.

719
01:26:23,494 --> 01:26:32,557
Orifice viewing may be via cystoscopy, if available; through a comorbid traumatic cystotomy; or through an intentional diagnostic cystotomy.

720
01:26:32,557 --> 01:26:38,944
For the last, a midline extraperitoneal cystotomy in the bladder dome gives excellent exposure.

721
01:26:38,944 --> 01:26:47,944
Sluggish or absent jets may reflect patient hypovolemia, and ongoing volume resuscitation may yield expected orifice jets.

722
01:26:47,944 --> 01:26:54,357
If not, consultation with a specialist is typically requested to exclude ureteral injury.

723
01:26:54,357 --> 01:26:58,094
While waiting, catheters and stents can be assembled.

724
01:26:58,094 --> 01:27:06,657
Semantically, ureteral catheters are diagnostic tools, and typically are inserted and removed in the same therapeutic intervention.

725
01:27:06,657 --> 01:27:11,794
Most stents are designed to remain indwelling for prolonged ureteral drainage.

726
01:27:11,794 --> 01:27:18,694
Both types are hollow to permit radiopaque medium injection and to allow urine egress through or around them .

727
01:27:18,694 --> 01:27:26,644
To exclude ureteral obstruction, a 4F to 6F open-ended or whistle-tip catheter is threaded into one orifice.

728
01:27:26,644 --> 01:27:31,869
Once inserted, the catheter is advanced to the level of suspected obstruction.

729
01:27:31,869 --> 01:27:37,244
If the tool threads easily up toward the renal pelvis, obstruction is unlikely.

730
01:27:37,244 --> 01:27:47,057
In most, ureteral injury occurs at or below the pelvic brim, and the distance from the ureteral orifice to the brim approximates 13 cm .

731
01:27:47,057 --> 01:27:53,882
Failure to easily advance the catheter may indicate ureteral kinking, ligation, or crush injury.

732
01:27:53,882 --> 01:28:00,032
The appearance of the catheter in the abdominal cavity indicates partial or complete transection .

733
01:28:00,032 --> 01:28:05,344
Repair of ureteral injuries is dependent upon the type of injury and location.

734
01:28:05,344 --> 01:28:11,132
First, kinked or ligated ureters can be relieved by release of ensnaring sutures.

735
01:28:11,132 --> 01:28:14,907
Crush injuries are inspected to ensure vital tissue.

736
01:28:14,907 --> 01:28:19,419
In these cases, stents are left to avert ureteral stricture.

737
01:28:19,419 --> 01:28:23,394
Ureteral stents range from 4F to 7F.

738
01:28:23,394 --> 01:28:32,544
Stents vary in length from 20 to 30 cm, and a 24-cm or 26-cm length is appropriate for most adults.

739
01:28:32,544 --> 01:28:42,332
Double-pigtail or double-J stents describe their tip shape, and the ends coil within the renal pelvis and bladder, respectively, to prevent stent migration .

740
01:28:42,332 --> 01:28:50,507
A Foley catheter remains for 7 to 10 days, and the ureteral stents are removed via cystoscopy after 14 days.

741
01:28:50,507 --> 01:29:01,244
Intravenous pyelography (IVP) is usually not necessary before removal of the stent if it was placed as a precautionary measure after relatively minor injury .

742
01:29:01,244 --> 01:29:08,457
Crush injuries with devascularization, thermal injury, or transection require more extensive repair.

743
01:29:08,457 --> 01:29:16,832
If a healthy-appearing ureter can be reimplanted into the bladder without undue tension, then ureteroneocystostomy is preferable.

744
01:29:16,832 --> 01:29:24,457
For more proximal injuries, a ureteroureterostomy, a psoas hitch, or a Boari flap may be needed.

745
01:29:24,457 --> 01:29:32,182
An explanation of these more extensive procedures is found in Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd edition .

746
01:29:32,182 --> 01:29:39,819
Unrecognized ureteral injury can mimic that of cystotomy with the addition of possible costovertebral angle tenderness.

747
01:29:39,819 --> 01:29:44,007
CT urography is a preferred initial diagnostic tool .

748
01:29:44,007 --> 01:29:48,732
The duration of time from injury to identification directs repair.

749
01:29:48,732 --> 01:29:53,232
Those identified early are often suitable for immediate repair.

750
01:29:53,232 --> 01:29:57,957
Bowel Injury Serosal tears represent weak points in the small bowel.

751
01:29:57,957 --> 01:30:04,644
If obstruction develops postoperatively, these weak spots may perforate, leading to peritonitis.

752
01:30:04,644 --> 01:30:11,369
If serosal tears are few, they can be oversewn with either a fine absorbable or nonabsorbable suture .

753
01:30:11,369 --> 01:30:16,119
More significant lacerations are often repaired in consultation

754
01:30:16,276 --> 01:30:20,326
with a general surgeon or gynecologic oncologist.

755
01:30:20,276 --> 01:30:22,363
Postoperative Care.

756
01:30:22,363 --> 01:30:25,413
# Euvolemia Evaluation.

757
01:30:25,413 --> 01:30:31,588
During and after cesarean delivery, requirements for IV fluids can vary considerably.

758
01:30:31,588 --> 01:30:35,726
Current ERAS strategies aim for euvolemic replacement .

759
01:30:35,726 --> 01:30:43,576
Administered fluids consist of either lactated Ringer solution or a similar crystalloid solution with 5-percent dextrose.

760
01:30:43,576 --> 01:30:49,551
Blood loss with uncomplicated cesarean delivery approximates 1000 mL.

761
01:30:49,551 --> 01:31:02,413
The averagesized woman with a hematocrit of 30 percent or more and with a normally expanded blood and extracellular fluid volume most often will tolerate blood loss up to 2000 mL without difficulty.

762
01:31:02,413 --> 01:31:11,876
Unappreciated bleeding through the vagina during the procedure, bleeding concealed in the uterus after its closure, or both commonly lead to underestimation.

763
01:31:11,876 --> 01:31:19,413
Blood loss averages 1500 mL with elective cesarean hysterectomy, although this is variable .

764
01:31:19,413 --> 01:31:26,488
Most peripartum hysterectomies are unscheduled, and blood loss in these cases is correspondingly greater.

765
01:31:26,488 --> 01:31:35,901
Thus, in addition to close monitoring of vital signs and urine output, the hematocrit should be determined intra- or postoperatively as indicated.

766
01:31:35,901 --> 01:31:38,351
# Recovery Suite.

767
01:31:38,351 --> 01:31:44,988
The amount of vaginal bleeding is closely monitored for at least an hour in the immediate postoperative period.

768
01:31:44,988 --> 01:31:52,438
The uterine fundus is also identified frequently by palpation to ensure that the uterus remains firmly contracted.

769
01:31:52,438 --> 01:32:00,563
Criteria for transfer to the postpartum ward include minimal bleeding, stable vital signs, and adequate urine output.

770
01:32:00,563 --> 01:32:03,876
# Hospital Care until Discharge.

771
01:32:03,876 --> 01:32:11,213
Analgesia, Vital Signs, Intravenous Fluids Several schemes are suitable for postoperative pain control.

772
01:32:11,213 --> 01:32:28,351
First, adding intrathecal opioid or epidural opioid such as morphine to neuraxial analgesia can provide 12 to 24 hours of postoperative relief (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019a; Caughey, 2018).

773
01:32:28,351 --> 01:32:34,013
Sedation and respiratory depression rise with increasing intrathecal opioid doses.

774
01:32:34,013 --> 01:32:41,401
Postoperative monitoring protocols reflect this and are outlined in Chapter 25 (p. 472) .

775
01:32:41,401 --> 01:32:46,538
Other potential side effects include pruritus, nausea, or vomiting .

776
01:32:46,538 --> 01:32:54,051
Despite these, long-acting neuraxial analgesia is recommended instead of intermittent parental opioid dosing .

777
01:32:54,051 --> 01:33:01,376
For additional relief, nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) can alternate with acetaminophen .

778
01:33:01,376 --> 01:33:07,863
Breakthrough pain can be relieved by oxycodone 2.5 to 5 mg every 4 hours .

779
01:33:07,863 --> 01:33:15,763
Neonatal sedation is a concern, and total oxycodone doses higher 30 mg/d are not recommended .

780
01:33:15,763 --> 01:33:30,063
Instead, for severe breakthrough pain, intramuscular (IM) meperidine, 50 to 75 mg every 3 to 4 hours, or IM morphine, 10 to 15 mg every 3 to 4 hours, is an option.

781
01:33:30,063 --> 01:33:39,676
For those without neuraxial anesthesia, a transversus abdominis place (TAP) block can be considered (Fig. 2-2, p. 14).

782
01:33:39,676 --> 01:33:44,451
Patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) also is reasonable.

783
01:33:44,451 --> 01:33:55,151
One PCA regimen uses IV morphine given as needed as a 1-mg dose with a 6-minute lockout interval and maximum dose of 30 mg in 4 hours.

784
01:33:55,151 --> 01:34:00,663
An additional 2-mg booster dose is permitted for a maximum of two doses.

785
01:34:00,663 --> 01:34:08,688
After transfer to her room, the woman is assessed at least hourly for 4 hours, and thereafter at intervals of 4 hours.

786
01:34:08,688 --> 01:34:13,138
Deep breathing and coughing are encouraged to prevent atelectasis.

787
01:34:13,138 --> 01:34:19,976
Vital signs, uterine tone, urine output, and vaginal and incisional bleeding are evaluated.

788
01:34:19,976 --> 01:34:24,113
The hematocrit is routinely measured the morning after surgery.

789
01:34:24,113 --> 01:34:34,126
It is checked sooner if blood loss was significant or if postoperative hypotension, tachycardia, oliguria, or other evidence suggests hypovolemia.

790
01:34:34,126 --> 01:34:42,076
If the hematocrit is significantly lower than the preoperative level, the measurement is repeated and a search for the cause is instituted.

791
01:34:42,076 --> 01:34:49,776
Clinical and objective thresholds for transfusion are described fully in Chapter 44 (p. 771).

792
01:34:49,776 --> 01:34:55,463
If ongoing blood loss is not expected, iron therapy is preferred to transfusion.

793
01:34:55,463 --> 01:35:02,826
Postpartum, the patient begins to mobilize and excrete her physiologically expanded extravascular volume.

794
01:35:02,826 --> 01:35:10,201
Thus, maintenance IV fluid proves adequate after surgery until consistent oral intake is reestablished.

795
01:35:10,201 --> 01:35:15,576
If urine output falls below 30 mL/hr, however, a woman

796
01:35:15,724 --> 01:35:18,511
should be reevaluated promptly.

797
01:35:18,461 --> 01:35:26,124
The cause of the oliguria may range from unrecognized blood loss to an antidiuretic effect from infused oxytocin.

798
01:35:26,124 --> 01:35:43,324
Women undergoing unscheduled cesarean delivery may have pathological retention or constriction of the extracellular fluid compartment caused by severe preeclampsia, sepsis syndrome, vomiting, prolonged labor without adequate fluid intake, or increased blood loss.

799
01:35:43,324 --> 01:35:48,836
Women with these complications are generally observed in the recovery room until improved.

800
01:35:48,836 --> 01:35:55,511
Bladder and Bowel Function A Foley catheter is often required to accurately assess urinary output.

801
01:35:55,511 --> 01:36:01,186
For women not needing such monitoring, organizations differ in their recommendations.

802
01:36:01,186 --> 01:36:13,199
The Society for Obstetric Anesthesia and Perinatology suggests removal after 6 to 12 hours and cites the urinary retention risks associated with long-acting neuraxial anesthesia .

803
01:36:13,199 --> 01:36:17,586
The ERAS Society recommends immediate postoperative removal .

804
01:36:17,586 --> 01:36:23,674
The prevalence of urinary retention following cesarean delivery approximates 5 percent .

805
01:36:23,674 --> 01:36:33,399
Failure to progress in labor and postoperative narcotic analgesia are identified risks (Chai, 2008; Liang, 2007).

806
01:36:33,399 --> 01:36:45,099
In uncomplicated cases, liquids or solid food may be offered within hours of surgery and advanced as tolerated (Guo, 2015; Macones, 2019).

807
01:36:45,099 --> 01:36:52,786
Some degree of adynamic ileus follows virtually every laparotomy but is negligible after most cesarean deliveries.

808
01:36:52,786 --> 01:37:00,261
Postoperative ileus symptoms include abdominal distention, colic, and an inability to pass flatus or stool.

809
01:37:00,261 --> 01:37:08,636
With persistent nausea and vomiting or with prolonged bowel function delay, radiological imaging may help exclude bowel obstruction.

810
01:37:08,636 --> 01:37:12,786
A plain abdominal radiograph is a frequent first choice.

811
01:37:12,786 --> 01:37:22,249
However, in the general population, this study is diagnostic in only 50 to 60 percent of small-bowel obstruction (SBO) cases .

812
01:37:22,249 --> 01:37:29,549
Thus, a radiograph may best serve as a triage tool in cases in which ileus is the suspected diagnosis.

813
01:37:29,549 --> 01:37:36,586
Notably, an enlarged postpartum uterus can compress the rectosigmoid and prevent it from filling with gas.

814
01:37:36,586 --> 01:37:43,661
Thus, findings suggesting a distal colonic obstruction may confuse true cases of transient ileus .

815
01:37:43,661 --> 01:37:49,849
CT with IV and oral contrast provides greater accuracy for SBO diagnosis.

816
01:37:49,849 --> 01:37:58,036
Last, although uncommon, an unrecognized bowel injury may underlie otherwise unexplained fever and bowel dysfunction.

817
01:37:58,036 --> 01:38:02,324
Again, CT would be the more diagnostic examination.

818
01:38:02,324 --> 01:38:08,811
For treatment of ileus, IV fluids compensate for poor oral intake and losses from emesis.

819
01:38:08,811 --> 01:38:14,711
Electrolyte imbalances are corrected to improve bowel motility and avoid bowel edema.

820
01:38:14,711 --> 01:38:20,949
Nasogastric decompression is necessary only with persistent vomiting or severe distention.

821
01:38:20,949 --> 01:38:31,199
For prevention, surgical goals strive to minimize bowel manipulation, avoid excess IV fluids or profound hypovolemia, and limit surgery length .

822
01:38:31,199 --> 01:38:36,074
After surgery, gum chewing enhances early bowel function recovery .

823
01:38:36,074 --> 01:38:46,386
Among studies, chewing was initiated immediately or up to 12 hours later, lasted 15 to 60 minutes, and was repeated in at least three sessions daily .

824
01:38:46,386 --> 01:38:56,249
Ambulation and Wound Care As discussed earlier, women undergoing cesarean delivery have an increased VTE risk compared with those delivering vaginally .

825
01:38:56,249 --> 01:38:59,236
Early ambulation lowers this risk.

826
01:38:59,236 --> 01:39:03,374
Walking to the bathroom begins, initially with assistance.

827
01:39:03,374 --> 01:39:05,774
Brief walks are encouraged.

828
01:39:05,774 --> 01:39:13,249
For women with marked abdominal laxity, an abdominal binder can aid comfortable deep coughing and ambulation.

829
01:39:13,249 --> 01:39:23,174
Evidence supporting routine application for all parturients is limited and conflicting (Chankhunaphas, 2020; Gillier, 2016).

830
01:39:23,174 --> 01:39:30,524
Although not evidence based, we remove the surgical dressing after 24 hours and inspect the incision daily.

831
01:39:30,524 --> 01:39:36,336
One small randomized trial showed no wound healing effects if removed at 6 hours .

832
01:39:36,336 --> 01:39:40,961
By the third postpartum day, showering does not harm the incision.

833
01:39:40,961 --> 01:39:45,986
Prior to this, a plastic covering can maintain dryness during showers.

834
01:39:45,986 --> 01:39:50,711
If used, staples are often removed on the fourth postoperative day.

835
01:39:50,711 --> 01:39:56,574
In their place, tape strips can remain for 1 week to reinforce skin edge integrity.

836
01:39:56,574 --> 01:40:04,361
If potential superficial wound separation, without infection, is a concern, staples can remain for 7 to 10

837
01:40:04,516 --> 01:40:06,028
days.

838
01:40:05,978 --> 01:40:08,691
# Hospital Discharge.

839
01:40:08,691 --> 01:40:15,278
For uncomplicated cesarean delivery, the average hospitalization length is 3 to 4 days .

840
01:40:15,278 --> 01:40:25,716
Data from studies suggest that earlier discharge is feasible for properly selected women and newborns (Bayoumi, 2016; Tan, 2012).

841
01:40:25,716 --> 01:40:31,041
Protocols ideally include earlier reevaluation for neonatal jaundice.

842
01:40:31,041 --> 01:40:37,278
However, discharge before 96 hours leads to a greater neonatal readmission rate .

843
01:40:37,278 --> 01:40:42,816
Activities during the first week are limited to self-care and newborn care with assistance.

844
01:40:42,816 --> 01:40:50,228
Driving can be resumed when pain does not limit the ability to brake quickly and when narcotic medications are not in use.

845
01:40:50,228 --> 01:41:01,191
In women with cesarean delivery, intercourse was resumed in 44 percent by 6 weeks postpartum, 81 percent by 3 months, and 97 percent at 1 year .

846
01:41:01,191 --> 01:41:16,528
After the puerperium, the quality of sexual functioning does not differ between those undergoing spontaneous vaginal birth or cesarean delivery (Chang, 2015; Fehniger, 2013; Rogers, 2014).

847
01:41:16,528 --> 01:41:19,028
Return to work is variable.

848
01:41:19,028 --> 01:41:28,103
Six weeks is commonly cited, although many women use the Family and Medical Leave Act to allow up to 12 weeks for recovery and newborn bonding.

